          1 => 'Each',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Recommended',
          1 => 'At least two per vehicle',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Placement',
          1 => 'Tires on the downhill side',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why chocks matter on a tree job',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Parking brakes fail, transmissions get bumped out of park, and ground shifts under a loaded truck. A bucket truck that rolls while someone is in the bucket, or a chip truck that rolls into the chipper crew, is a catastrophic accident that a pair of chocks prevents. That is why most companies and many standards require chocks on any vehicle that is parked for work.',
            1 => 'Wheel chocks also tell everyone on site that the truck is set up and not moving. Make placing them part of your arrival routine, along with cones, signs and setting up the work zone.',
            2 => 'Tree trucks are often parked on residential streets with a crown, on driveways with a slope, or on soft shoulders. Each of those increases the chance of a vehicle creeping, especially once the chipper is running and the truck is vibrating.',
            3 => 'Chocks matter for chippers too. A towed chipper parked on a slope with the truck unhooked has nothing holding it but its own jack, and a rolling chipper is as dangerous as a rolling truck.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How to chock the wheels correctly',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Set the parking brake first. Then place the chocks snug against the tires on the downhill side, so gravity pushes the tire into the chock. On level ground, chock both front and back of one tire. On a slope, chock the downhill side of both rear tires, or follow your company and vehicle maker\'s procedures if they call for more.',
            1 => 'For bucket trucks, chock before you set the outriggers and do not pull the chocks until the boom is stowed and the outriggers are up. On a trailer or chipper, chock the trailer wheels before unhitching. Always remove the chocks before you drive off. A lot of crews hang a tag on the steering wheel as a reminder.',
            2 => 'Train everyone on the crew to do it, not just the driver. When chocking is a habit the whole crew owns, it does not get skipped on the rushed jobs, which are exactly the ones that go wrong.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing a wheel chock',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Pick a chock sized for your tire diameter and vehicle weight. Small chocks made for cars or campers will roll right over on a heavy truck. Rubber and polyurethane chocks grip well on pavement and do not split. A handle or rope makes them easy to pull out from under a truck. Bright colors help you find them and help others see them.',
            1 => 'Keep them in a fixed spot on the truck so they are always easy to grab. See more <a href="/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/bucket-truck-accessories/">bucket truck accessories</a>, including <a href="/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/bucket-truck-accessories/bigfoot-composite-outrigger-pads/">outrigger pads</a> for soft ground.',
            2 => 'Inspect chocks now and then for cracks, chunks missing from the face and worn handles, and replace them when they no longer sit flat against the tire.',
            3 => 'Some crews pair a chock with a small cone so the chocked wheel is easy to spot from the cab before driving away.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How many wheel chocks should a truck have?',
          'a' => 'At least two. Many crews carry more for trailers, chippers and work on steep grades.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Which side of the tire do chocks go on?',
          'a' => 'On the downhill side, so the tire would roll into the chock. On level ground, chock both sides of one tire.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I still need chocks if the parking brake is set?',
          'a' => 'Yes. Chocks are a backup for brake or transmission failure, and many company policies require both.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'bucket',
        1 => 'safety',
        2 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/traffic-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/traffic-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Traffic Cone',
        'creator' => 'pdugmore2001',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/78528924@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/78528924@N00/6173960958',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-scabbards/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-scabbards/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/',
      'name' => 'Bucket Truck Chainsaw Scabbard',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'case-bag',
      'price' => 59,
      'keyword' => 'chainsaw scabbard for bucket truck',
      'title' => 'Chainsaw Scabbard for Bucket Truck and Aerial Lifts',
      'h1' => 'Chainsaw Scabbard for Bucket Truck and Aerial Lifts',
      'meta' => 'A chainsaw scabbard for bucket truck and aerial lift work holds your saw on the bucket lip, ready to grab and out of your way. Fit, mounting and safety tips.',
      'tagline' => 'A bucket-mounted saw holder that keeps a chainsaw secure and in reach aloft.',
      'summary' => 'A chainsaw scabbard for bucket truck work hangs on the bucket lip and holds your saw bar-down, so it is ready when you need it and out of the way when you do not. It keeps the saw off the bucket floor, away from your legs, and out of the controls.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Hangs on the side of the bucket',
        1 => 'Holds the saw bar-down and secure',
        2 => 'Keeps the saw off the floor and away from controls',
        3 => 'Fits top-handle and small rear-handle saws (check bar length)',
        4 => 'Drain openings so sawdust and water fall through',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Bucket truck chainsaw scabbard',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Mount',
          1 => 'Hooks over the bucket lip',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Saw position',
          1 => 'Bar down',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fits',
          1 => 'Top-handle and compact saws',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Bucket trucks and aerial lifts',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a scabbard belongs on every bucket',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A bucket is a small space. Two people, a saw, a pole saw, a hand saw and maybe a rope all share it. A running or recently stopped chainsaw lying on the floor is a cut risk to your legs and can bump into the controls. A bucket truck chainsaw scabbard solves that. The saw hangs outside the working space, bar down, and you can grab it with one hand.',
            1 => 'It also protects the saw. Saws knocking around on the bucket floor get chains dulled and covers cracked. In the scabbard, the chain is shielded and the saw is not rattling around while you move the boom.',
            2 => 'On the ground, the same scabbard keeps the saw secure on the truck between bucket setups, and it means the saw always lives in the same place, which helps every crew member find it without looking.',
            3 => 'It also gives your hands back. With the saw hung outside the bucket, you have both hands for positioning the boom, handling a pole saw or tying off a piece for a controlled lower.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit and mounting',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check that the scabbard fits the lip of your bucket and the saw you run. Most work with top-handle saws and compact rear-handle saws, but bar length and powerhead size vary, so compare the listing with your saw. Mount it on the side away from the controls and where it will not catch on limbs as you boom through the canopy.',
            1 => 'Some crews run two: one for the top-handle and one for a pole saw or hand saw. Keep the chainsaw holder for the bucket truck clear of hydraulic lines and the platform gate.',
            2 => 'If you run a two-man bucket, agree on which side the saw lives on so both operators know where it is and nobody reaches across the controls for it.',
            3 => 'A bucket truck chainsaw scabbard with drain openings stays lighter and cleaner, since rain and sawdust fall through rather than collecting around the bar.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Safe habits aloft',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Always engage the chain brake before putting the saw in the scabbard, and never put a running saw away. In the bucket, follow ANSI Z133 and your employer\'s procedures, wear your fall protection attached to the designated anchor, and keep a safe distance from energized lines unless you are a qualified line-clearance arborist.',
            1 => 'Clean the scabbard out regularly so sawdust and sap do not pack in. See related gear in <a href="/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/bucket-truck-accessories/">bucket truck accessories</a> and <a href="/jobsite/chainsaw-accessories/chainsaw-cases-and-scabbards/the-classic-carrying-case-by-husqvarna/">chainsaw cases</a>.',
            2 => 'Check the scabbard for cracks, worn hooks and loose hardware during your daily truck inspection. A scabbard that pops off the bucket lip drops a saw on whoever is below, so replace it as soon as it no longer seats firmly. Keep the work zone below the bucket clear of ground workers whenever you are cutting or handling the saw aloft.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will a bucket truck scabbard fit my saw?',
          'a' => 'Most fit top-handle and compact saws. Check the bar length and powerhead size against the listing.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I mount a chainsaw scabbard on a bucket?',
          'a' => 'It hooks over the bucket lip. Place it away from the controls and where it will not snag on branches.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I put the saw away with the engine running?',
          'a' => 'No. Shut the saw off, engage the chain brake, then put it in the scabbard.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'bucket',
        1 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Climbing Gear for Mt. Fuji',
        'creator' => 'KE-TA',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/41894154003@N01',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/41894154003@N01/1262155760',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/fall-arrest-for-aerial-lifts/6-web-retractable-fall-protection-lanyard/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/fall-arrest-for-aerial-lifts/6-web-retractable-fall-protection-lanyard/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/fall-arrest-for-aerial-lifts/',
      'name' => '6\' Web Retractable Fall Protection Lanyard',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'lanyard',
      'price' => 99,
      'keyword' => 'retractable safety harness',
      'title' => 'Retractable Safety Harness Lanyard, 6\' Web',
      'h1' => '6\' Web Retractable Safety Harness Lanyard',
      'meta' => 'A 6-foot web retractable lanyard to pair with your retractable safety harness setup. Where it works, where it does not, and the checks to run before each use.',
      'tagline' => 'A 6-foot self-retracting web lanyard for fall protection at height.',
      'summary' => 'A retractable lanyard is the piece that turns a fall arrest harness into a retractable safety harness system: the web pays out as you move and locks if you fall. This 6-foot web unit keeps slack off the floor while letting you move around your work area.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '6-foot self-retracting web lifeline',
        1 => 'Takes up slack automatically as you move',
        2 => 'Locks quickly in a fall',
        3 => 'Compact and light compared with cable units',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Length',
          1 => '6 ft',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Lifeline',
          1 => 'Webbing',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Self-retracting lanyard',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where a retractable lanyard fits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A retractable lanyard harness setup is common on rooftops, platforms and structures where you move around an anchor above you. Because it keeps the line short, it limits free fall distance compared with a standard shock-absorbing lanyard.',
            1 => 'In aerial lifts, check carefully before using any self-retracting device. Bucket anchorages are often at or below the waist, and many retractables are rated only for overhead anchorage. Use only equipment the lift manufacturer and the device\'s instructions allow for that use.',
            2 => 'A web lifeline is lighter and less bulky than a cable unit, and it is easier on surfaces it rubs against.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and training',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Before each use, pull the web out fully and check it for cuts, burns and fraying, test that it locks with a sharp tug, and check the housing and connectors. Remove from service any unit that has arrested a fall or fails a check.',
            1 => 'Fall protection must meet the applicable standard, be used per the manufacturer\'s instructions and ANSI Z133 where it applies, and be used by trained workers. See other <a href="/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/fall-arrest-for-aerial-lifts/">fall arrest gear for aerial lifts</a>.',
            2 => 'Keep the housing clean, store it out of the weather and never let the web retract uncontrolled.',
            3 => 'Read the label for the device\'s capacity and the anchorage it is rated for before first use.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a retractable lanyard in a bucket truck?',
          'a' => 'Only if the device is rated for the anchorage position in your bucket and the lift maker allows it. Many are rated for overhead anchorage only.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What harness do I use with it?',
          'a' => 'A full-body fall arrest harness, connected at the attachment point specified in the instructions.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'safety',
        1 => 'bucket',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/lanyard-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/lanyard-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Fall protection field trials',
        'creator' => 'Western Area Power Admin',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05/15137306027',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/fall-arrest-for-aerial-lifts/petzl-absorbica-i/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/fall-arrest-for-aerial-lifts/petzl-absorbica-i/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/fall-arrest-for-aerial-lifts/',
      'name' => 'Petzl ABSORBICA-I Energy Absorbing Lanyard',
      'brand' => 'petzl',
      'brand_name' => 'Petzl',
      'kind' => 'lanyard',
      'price' => 99,
      'keyword' => 'petzl absorbica',
      'title' => 'Petzl ABSORBICA-I Energy Absorbing Lanyard',
      'h1' => 'Petzl ABSORBICA-I',
      'meta' => 'Petzl ABSORBICA-I is a single energy-absorbing lanyard for fall arrest. Lengths, connectors, how it limits fall forces and when to remove it from service.',
      'tagline' => 'A single-leg energy-absorbing lanyard for fall arrest at height.',
      'summary' => 'The Petzl ABSORBICA-I is a single-leg lanyard with a built-in energy absorber. In a fall, the absorber tears open to cut the force on your body and your anchor. The Petzl Absorbica line is compact and light, which makes it easy to carry on a full-body harness.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Single-leg fall arrest lanyard',
        1 => 'Built-in energy absorber limits fall forces',
        2 => 'Available in different lengths and connector options',
        3 => 'Compact absorber pack',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Energy-absorbing lanyard',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Legs',
          1 => 'Single',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Lengths',
          1 => '80 cm or 150 cm (check listing)',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Petzl',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How it works',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'An energy absorber is a pack of webbing stitched so it rips apart in a controlled way during a fall. That spreads the stopping force out over a short distance, reducing the peak load on your body. After it deploys, the lanyard is done and must be retired.',
            1 => 'Absorber lanyards need clearance below you to work, since the absorber extends during a fall. Check the clearance requirements in Petzl\'s instructions before you anchor.',
            2 => 'Choose the length to suit your setup. A shorter lanyard limits how far you can move from the anchor but also reduces fall distance.',
            3 => 'Plan your anchor position so the lanyard is never slack across an edge, and never tie a knot in it to shorten it.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Aerial lift use and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'In aerial lifts, use the anchorage, harness and lanyard combination allowed by the lift manufacturer and your employer, and check the lanyard\'s length against the platform. Inspect before each use: webbing, stitching, the absorber pack, and connectors. Any sign of deployment means retirement.',
            1 => 'Fall protection must meet the applicable standard and be used per the manufacturer\'s instructions and ANSI Z133 by trained workers. See more <a href="/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/fall-arrest-for-aerial-lifts/">fall arrest gear for aerial lifts</a>.',
            2 => 'Store the lanyard dry and away from chemicals and sharp gear, and record when it entered service.',
            3 => 'Check that the connectors gate fully and lock every time.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I reuse an ABSORBICA after a fall?',
          'a' => 'No. Once the absorber has deployed, even partly, the lanyard must be removed from service.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the ABSORBICA-I a positioning lanyard?',
          'a' => 'No. It is a fall arrest lanyard. Use a dedicated adjustable lanyard for work positioning.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'safety',
        1 => 'bucket',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/petzl/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/lanyard-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/lanyard-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Arborist climbing on the tree',
        'creator' => 'Yuliya Krasylenko',
        'creator_url' => 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:Yuliya_Krasylenko',
        'license' => 'CC BY 4.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=97167024',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/fall-arrest-for-aerial-lifts/safewaze-economy-fall-protection-kit/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/fall-arrest-for-aerial-lifts/safewaze-economy-fall-protection-kit/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/fall-arrest-for-aerial-lifts/',
      'name' => 'Safewaze Economy Fall Protection Kit',
      'brand' => 'safewaze',
      'brand_name' => 'Safewaze',
      'kind' => 'saddle',
      'price' => 89,
      'keyword' => 'safewaze fall protection',
      'title' => 'Safewaze Fall Protection Economy Kit',
      'h1' => 'Safewaze Fall Protection Economy Kit',
      'meta' => 'Safewaze fall protection economy kit: a basic harness and lanyard package for aerial lifts and general work at height. What to check, fit and training notes.',
      'tagline' => 'A budget-friendly harness and lanyard kit for aerial lift crews.',
      'summary' => 'This Safewaze fall protection kit packages the basics for working from an aerial lift or other elevated platform at an economy price. It is a sensible way to outfit a new crew member or keep spare sets on the truck.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Harness and lanyard package',
        1 => 'Economy price for outfitting crews',
        2 => 'Good for spare sets on the truck',
        3 => 'From Safewaze, a fall protection maker',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Fall protection kit',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Safewaze',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Aerial lifts, general work at height',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What a kit is good for',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Buying a matched harness and lanyard together means the pieces are designed to work together and you are not guessing at compatibility. For companies adding bucket trucks or lifts, or putting new hires in the bucket, an economy kit gets everyone properly equipped without a long shopping list.',
            1 => 'Check the kit contents and the lanyard type in the listing, and make sure they suit how your crew uses lifts.',
            2 => 'Because the price is lower, there is less temptation to keep using a harness that should have been retired. Replace worn gear rather than stretching it.',
            3 => 'Keep a written log of each kit\'s inspections.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit, inspection and training',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A harness only works when it fits. Adjust leg straps and chest strap snug, with the back D-ring between the shoulder blades. Inspect webbing, stitching, buckles, D-rings and the lanyard before every use, and retire anything that has arrested a fall or shows damage.',
            1 => 'Fall protection must meet the applicable standard, be used per the manufacturer\'s instructions and ANSI Z133 where it applies, and be used by trained workers. Compare options in <a href="/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/fall-arrest-for-aerial-lifts/">fall arrest for aerial lifts</a>.',
            2 => 'Label each kit and assign it to one worker when you can, so fit adjustments stay set and inspections are easy to track.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is this kit suitable for tree climbing?',
          'a' => 'No. It is fall arrest gear for platforms and lifts. Tree climbing requires an arborist saddle and climbing system.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How often should I replace a harness?',
          'a' => 'Follow the manufacturer\'s service life guidance and retire it right away after a fall or any damage.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'safety',
        1 => 'bucket',
      ),
      'level' => 'beginner',
      'brand_path' => '/safewaze/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/saddle-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/saddle-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Tree Surgeon',
        'creator' => 'Tim Sheerman-Chase',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/68932647@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/68932647@N00/7738607258',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/ground-protection-mats/ground-protection-mats-3x8/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/ground-protection-mats/ground-protection-mats-3x8/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/ground-protection-mats/',
      'name' => 'Ground Protection Mats, 3\' x 8\'',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'jobsite',
      'price' => 249,
      'keyword' => 'hdpe ground protection mats',
      'title' => 'HDPE Ground Protection Mats, 3\' x 8\'',
      'h1' => 'HDPE Ground Protection Mats, 3\' x 8\'',
      'meta' => 'HDPE ground protection mats in a 3x8 size that one or two people can handle. Protect lawns and soft ground under trucks and loaders. Specs and use tips.',
      'tagline' => 'A 3 x 8 foot HDPE mat for protecting turf and soft ground on tree jobs.',
      'summary' => 'HDPE ground protection mats lay down a temporary road for trucks, loaders and stump grinders, spreading the load so you do not leave ruts in a client\'s lawn. The 3 x 8 foot size is easy for a crew to carry and place.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '3 x 8 ft high-density polyethylene mat',
        1 => 'Spreads load from trucks and compact equipment',
        2 => 'Traction tread for tires and tracks',
        3 => 'Easy for a crew to carry and place',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Size',
          1 => '3 x 8 ft',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'HDPE',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Turf and soft ground protection',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why mats pay for themselves',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Repairing ruts in a lawn or replacing broken pavers costs money and costs you the client. HDPE ground mats stop that before it starts. They spread the weight of a mini skid steer, a stump grinder or a chip truck tire over a larger area, so the ground underneath stays intact.',
            1 => 'The 3 x 8 ground protection mats size is a handy one. It is light enough for one or two people to move, narrow enough for gates and side yards, and you can lay several in a line to build a path.',
            2 => 'Lay them across a lawn to get a chipper or mini loader to the back of the property, or under the tires of a truck parked on soft ground.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Laying and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Lay mats on ground that is as level as possible, overlap or connect them so edges do not lift, and run tires down the center. Check the load rating against your heaviest machine.',
            1 => 'Hose them off before loading so you do not track mud onto the next job, and stack them flat. See the full range of <a href="/jobsite/bucket-truck-and-aerial-lift-gear/ground-protection-mats/ground-protection-mats/">ground protection mats</a>.',
            2 => 'Inspect for cracks and torn connector holes before laying them, and replace damaged mats so edges do not catch tires or boots.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can a bucket truck drive on 3x8 mats?',
          'a' => 'Check the mat\'s load rating against your truck\'s axle weight. Many crews use larger or thicker mats for heavy trucks.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do HDPE mats work on wet ground?',
          'a' => 'Yes. That is when they help most, since soft ground ruts easily.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/jobsite-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/jobsite-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Cat Rescue',
        'creator' => 'juhansonin',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/38869431@N00',
