        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'A climbing saw for pruners',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Pruning climbers make hundreds of small cuts a day. A heavy saw on the saddle wears you down long before the work is done. The Husqvarna T525 solves that with a 27 cc engine and a power head that weighs about 5.9 lb. It is one of the lightest gas climbing saws you can buy, and that is the whole point.',
            1 => 'With a 10 or 12-inch bar, it handles deadwood, crown cleaning, reduction cuts and small limbs with ease. It is not the saw for big wood aloft. When you get into larger limbs, send up a bigger top-handle saw or a rear-handle saw on a line.',
            2 => 'That low weight also means less fatigue on your arms and back over a long pruning day, which helps you make better cuts late in the afternoon when attention starts to slip.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a top handle chainsaw',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Top handle chain saws put the rear handle on top of the engine, which shortens the saw and lets you hold it close in a working position. That compact shape is what makes them practical in a tree, where you often cut from awkward positions. The T525 adds a lanyard attachment point so the saw is always tied to your saddle.',
            1 => 'As an arborist chainsaw, the T525 shines on pruning jobs, utility clearance trims and fine work in ornamental trees. Many climbers carry it alongside a good pruning hand saw so they can switch between powered cuts and fine handsaw cuts without changing position.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Safe use aloft',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Top handle chainsaws are for trained arborists working aloft. Follow ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions on holding and starting the saw. Use two hands whenever possible, keep the saw on a saw lanyard, and be aware of where your climbing line and lanyard are at all times. Never use a top-handle saw on the ground for general cutting.',
            1 => 'Wear a helmet with eye and hearing protection and gloves. Plan every cut so the limb falls clear of you and your system. Get proper training before you take any climbing saw up a tree.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Maintenance and who should buy it',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Keep the chain sharp, use fresh 50:1 fuel and clean the air filter often. Small engines are sensitive to dirty filters and old fuel. Replace the spark plug and fuel filter regularly, and check the chain brake every day.',
            1 => 'Buy the T525 if your work is mostly pruning and you want the lightest gas saw on your saddle. If you also do removals aloft, compare the <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-t540xp-mark-iii-top-handle-chainsaw/">Husqvarna T540XP Mark III</a> for more power and longer bars.',
            2 => 'Some climbers also keep a T525 as a second saw on removal jobs, for small limbs and cleanup cuts after the bigger saw has done the heavy work. Its light weight makes it easy to carry alongside other gear.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How light is the Husqvarna T525?',
          'a' => 'The power head weighs about 5.9 lb, which makes it one of the lightest gas top-handle saws available.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What bar length fits the T525?',
          'a' => 'It is usually run with a 10 or 12 in bar, suited to pruning and small limbs.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the T525 a good arborist chainsaw for removals?',
          'a' => 'It is best for pruning. For removal work aloft, most climbers prefer a stronger top-handle saw such as the T540XP.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Who should use a top handle chainsaw?',
          'a' => 'Trained arborists working aloft. For ground work, use a rear-handle saw.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'pruning',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/husqvarna/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/top-handle-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/top-handle-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Tree Service Photography',
        'creator' => 'jacobavanzato',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/98915111@N02',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/98915111@N02/15991012910',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-t540xp-mark-iii-top-handle-chainsaw/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-t540xp-mark-iii-top-handle-chainsaw/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/',
      'name' => 'Husqvarna T540XP Mark III Top Handle Chainsaw',
      'brand' => 'husqvarna',
      'brand_name' => 'Husqvarna',
      'kind' => 'top-handle',
      'price' => 679,
      'keyword' => 'husqvarna t540xp',
      'title' => 'Husqvarna T540XP Mark III Top Handle Saw: Specs',
      'h1' => 'Husqvarna T540XP Mark III Top Handle Chainsaw',
      'meta' => 'Husqvarna T540XP Mark III top handle chainsaw: specs, bar sizes, how it runs on removals and pruning aloft, plus safety notes for climbing saws.',
      'tagline' => 'Husqvarna\'s pro top-handle saw for removals and heavy pruning aloft.',
      'summary' => 'The Husqvarna T540XP Mark III is Husqvarna\'s professional top-handle chainsaw, built for climbers who do removal work and heavy pruning aloft. Its 37.7 cc engine gives it enough power for bigger limbs while staying compact enough to work from a saddle.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '37.7 cc engine, the largest in Husqvarna\'s gas top-handle line',
        1 => 'AutoTune carburetor adjustment',
        2 => 'Runs 12 to 16 in bars',
        3 => 'Lanyard attachment point for tying in',
        4 => 'Built for daily professional climbing work',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Displacement',
          1 => '37.7 cc',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Power',
          1 => 'about 2.4 hp (1.8 kW)',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Recommended bar',
          1 => '12 to 16 in',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Chain pitch',
          1 => '3/8 in low profile',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Carburetor',
          1 => 'AutoTune',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fuel',
          1 => '50:1 gas and two-stroke oil mix',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'The removal climber\'s top-handle saw',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Pruning saws are about weight. Removal saws are about power. The Husqvarna T540XP is aimed squarely at the climber who takes trees down piece by piece and needs a top-handle saw that can cut real wood. With a 37.7 cc engine, the husqvarna t540xp chainsaw handles larger limbs and smaller spar sections that a 25 cc saw would struggle with.',
            1 => 'The Mark III is the latest version of the husqvarna t540 xp. It builds on the earlier models\' power and handling and keeps the features professional climbers rely on, including AutoTune and a lanyard attachment point.',
            2 => 'That extra power also helps on pruning jobs with large reduction cuts, where a smaller saw would bog in the middle of the cut. Many removal climbers use it as their only saw aloft and only send up a rear-handle saw for the biggest wood.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Power and setup',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The T540XP runs bars from 12 to 16 inches. A 14-inch bar is a common all-round choice. A 16-inch bar adds reach for bigger limbs and spar work but makes the saw a little less nimble. Use low-profile 3/8 in chain that matches your bar.',
            1 => 'AutoTune keeps the carburetor adjusted for fuel, altitude, temperature and air filter condition. On a saw that might sit on a saddle for an hour and then cut hard for ten minutes, that consistency matters. It starts and runs the same way every time.',
            2 => 'Searches for husqvarna 540xp and husqvarna t540 often come from climbers comparing generations. The core idea has stayed the same: a light, powerful top-handle saw for pros. Check the bar mount and chain spec before reusing bars from an older saw.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Safety aloft',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Top-handle saws are for trained arborists working aloft. Follow ANSI Z133 and the operator\'s manual. Use two hands whenever possible, keep the saw on a saw lanyard, and know where your climbing line, lanyard and body are relative to the bar. A powerful top-handle saw cuts through rope as easily as wood.',
            1 => 'Plan removal cuts carefully. Know where the piece will go, how it will be rigged and where the ground crew is. Wear a helmet with eye and hearing protection and gloves, and never use a top-handle saw on the ground for general cutting.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Maintenance and comparisons',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Keep the chain sharp and clean the air filter often. Use fresh 50:1 fuel, replace the spark plug and fuel filter regularly and check the chain brake daily. A top-handle saw takes a lot of knocks in the tree, so inspect the lanyard ring, handle and chain catcher regularly.',
            1 => 'If your work is mostly pruning, the lighter <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-t525-top-handle-chainsaw/">Husqvarna T525</a> may be a better fit. If you want a mid-range option, compare the <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-t435-top-handle-chainsaw/">T435</a>. For removal climbers, the T540XP is the one to beat in Husqvarna\'s lineup.',
            2 => 'Keep a spare plug and a couple of sharp chains in your climbing kit bag. A quick chain change on the ground between climbs keeps the saw cutting the way it should.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the displacement of the Husqvarna T540XP?',
          'a' => 'It has a 37.7 cc engine, the largest in Husqvarna\'s gas top-handle range.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What bar length should I run on a T540XP?',
          'a' => 'It runs 12 to 16 in bars. A 14 in bar is a popular all-round choice.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does the T540XP Mark III have AutoTune?',
          'a' => 'Yes. AutoTune adjusts the carburetor automatically for changing conditions.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the T540XP good for pruning?',
          'a' => 'It works for pruning, but it is built for power. Climbers who mostly prune often prefer a lighter saw like the T525.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'pruning',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/husqvarna/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/top-handle-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/top-handle-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'David and Goliath',
        'creator' => 'opticalreflex',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/26922258@N02',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/26922258@N02/3094850112',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/husqvarna-replacement-fuel-filter-for-t435-chainsaw/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/husqvarna-replacement-fuel-filter-for-t435-chainsaw/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/',
      'name' => 'Husqvarna Replacement Fuel Filter for T435 Chainsaw',
      'brand' => 'husqvarna',
      'brand_name' => 'Husqvarna',
      'kind' => 'parts',
      'price' => 10,
      'keyword' => 'husqvarna chainsaw fuel filter',
      'title' => 'Husqvarna Chainsaw Fuel Filter for the T435',
      'h1' => 'Husqvarna Chainsaw Fuel Filter for the T435',
      'meta' => 'Husqvarna chainsaw fuel filter for the T435 top-handle saw. Why it matters, when to replace it, how to tell a clogged filter and tips to avoid fuel problems.',
      'tagline' => 'Replacement in-tank fuel filter for the Husqvarna T435 top-handle saw.',
      'summary' => 'This Husqvarna chainsaw fuel filter is the in-tank replacement filter for the T435 top-handle saw. It keeps dirt and debris in the tank from reaching the carburetor, and it is one of the cheapest ways to keep a climbing saw starting and running well.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Replacement in-tank fuel filter for the Husqvarna T435',
        1 => 'Keeps debris out of the carburetor',
        2 => 'Inexpensive part worth keeping in the saw box',
        3 => 'Simple to swap with a hooked wire or fuel line tool',
        4 => 'Part of a regular tune-up with a new plug and air filter',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fits',
          1 => 'Husqvarna T435 top-handle chainsaw',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'In-tank fuel pickup filter',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Husqvarna',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Installation',
          1 => 'Inside the fuel tank on the end of the fuel line',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Service',
          1 => 'Replace at least once a season or per manual',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why the fuel filter matters',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Every chainsaw has a small filter on the end of the fuel line, sitting in the bottom of the tank. Its job is simple: stop dirt, sawdust and bits of debris from getting sucked into the carburetor. On a top-handle saw like the T435, which gets refueled on the ground, in the bucket and sometimes in less than tidy conditions, that filter works hard.',
            1 => 'When the filter gets clogged, the engine starves for fuel. The saw may start and idle fine, then bog or die when you hit the throttle. It may run for a minute and quit, or lose power in the middle of a cut. Those symptoms often get blamed on the carburetor, when a ten-dollar filter is the real problem.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'When to replace it',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Husqvarna\'s operator manual gives service intervals, and replacing the fuel filter at least once a season is a good habit for most users. Climbers who run the saw every day may want to change it more often. Replace it any time you see the saw starving for fuel, after running a tank of dirty or old fuel, or when you service the saw after storage.',
            1 => 'It is also smart to keep a spare in the saw box. A fuel filter weighs almost nothing and can save a day\'s work if a saw starts acting up on a job far from the shop.',
            2 => 'A fuel filter for Husqvarna saws is model-specific in some cases, so order the one listed for your saw rather than a universal part when you can. A filter that does not fit the fuel line snugly can slip off in the tank, and then you are pulling the line back out on the tailgate.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How to change it',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'With the saw cool and on a clean surface, drain the tank and remove the fuel cap. Use a hooked wire or a small fuel line tool to fish the fuel line out of the tank, being careful not to stretch or tear the line. Pull the old filter off the end of the line, push the new one on firmly and drop it back into the tank so it sits at the bottom.',
            1 => 'While you are in there, look at the fuel line. If it is hard, cracked or swollen, replace it too. A cracked fuel line will let air in and cause the same running problems as a clogged filter.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Keep fuel problems away',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Most fuel filter problems start with the fuel itself. Mix fresh fuel in a clean can, use quality two-stroke oil, and do not leave mixed fuel sitting for months. Pre-mixed fuel is a good option for saws that sit between jobs. Keep the area around the fuel cap clean before you open it so sawdust does not fall into the tank.',
            1 => 'Pair a new filter with a fresh spark plug and a clean air filter for a full tune-up. See the <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-t435-top-handle-chainsaw/">Husqvarna T435</a> page for more on the saw, or browse more <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/">chainsaw parts</a>.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I know if my chainsaw fuel filter is clogged?',
          'a' => 'Common signs are a saw that bogs or dies under throttle, loses power in the cut or will not stay running after starting. Replacing the filter is a quick, low-cost first check.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does this filter fit other Husqvarna saws?',
          'a' => 'This listing is for the T435. Fuel filters differ between models, so check your operator\'s manual or parts list before ordering for another saw.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How often should I replace the fuel filter?',
          'a' => 'At least once a season for most users, or more often for saws that run every day. Follow the interval in your manual.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/husqvarna/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/parts-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/parts-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'chainsaw',
        'creator' => 'sf-dvs',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/46207792@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/46207792@N00/64064283',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/ngk-bpmr7a-spark-plug/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/ngk-bpmr7a-spark-plug/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/',
      'name' => 'NGK BPMR7A Spark Plug',
      'brand' => 'ngk',
      'brand_name' => 'NGK',
      'kind' => 'spark-plug',
      'price' => 5,
      'keyword' => 'ngk bpmr7a',
      'title' => 'NGK BPMR7A Spark Plug: Specs, Fitment & Tips',
      'h1' => 'NGK BPMR7A Spark Plug',
      'meta' => 'NGK BPMR7A spark plug for chainsaws and small engines. Thread and hex size, what the code means, gap and fitment tips, and when to replace it.',
      'tagline' => 'A common resistor spark plug for chainsaws and small two-stroke engines.',
      'summary' => 'The NGK BPMR7A is one of the most common spark plugs in chainsaws and small outdoor power equipment, a 14 mm resistor plug with a projected tip. If your saw, blower or trimmer calls for a BPMR7A spark plug, keeping a couple in the truck is cheap insurance against hard starting.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '14 mm thread, 13/16 in (20.8 mm) hex',
        1 => 'Resistor type to reduce electrical interference',
        2 => 'Projected insulator tip',
        3 => 'Heat range 7 in NGK\'s numbering',
        4 => 'Widely used in chainsaws, blowers and small engines',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Thread',
          1 => '14 mm',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Hex',
          1 => '13/16 in (20.8 mm)',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Reach',
          1 => '3/8 in (9.5 mm)',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Resistor',
          1 => 'Yes',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Heat range',
          1 => '7 (NGK)',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Tip',
          1 => 'Projected insulator',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What the BPMR7A code means',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'NGK spark plug codes look cryptic, but they tell you a lot. In the bpmr7a, the B means a 14 mm thread with a 13/16 in hex. P means a projected insulator tip. M marks a compact plug design with a short reach. R means it has a built-in resistor. The 7 is the heat range, and the A is a design variation.',
            1 => 'The resistor matters on modern equipment. It cuts down on electrical noise from the ignition, which can affect electronic ignition modules and other electronics. If your manual calls for the resistor version, use it. The ngk bpm7a spark plug is the non-resistor version of the same basic plug, so do not swap them unless your manual allows it.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where you will find it',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The BPMR7A is found in a wide range of chainsaws, blowers, trimmers and other small two-stroke engines from several manufacturers. That is part of why the ngk bpmr7a spark plug is such a common search: lots of people own something that uses it.',
            1 => 'Do not guess on fitment. Check the operator\'s manual or the plug that came out of the engine. Heat range, reach and thread all matter. A plug with the wrong reach can hit the piston or leave threads exposed to carbon, and the wrong heat range can cause fouling or overheating.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Gap, install and replacement',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Set the gap to the figure in your engine\'s manual using a wire gauge, and do not assume the gap out of the box is correct for your engine. Thread the plug in by hand first to avoid cross-threading, then snug it with a plug wrench. Do not over-tighten, especially in aluminum cylinder heads.',
            1 => 'Replace the plug on the schedule in your manual or any time it looks worn, fouled or oily. A black, sooty plug can point to a rich mixture or a dirty air filter. A white, blistered plug can point to a lean condition that needs attention before it damages the engine. Always replace a plug with the engine cool and the plug wire or boot removed, and never test for spark by holding the plug near the open spark plug hole. Fuel vapor can ignite.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Keep spares on the truck',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A spark plug is the cheapest part in the saw box and one of the first things to check when a saw will not start. Many crews keep a few bpmr7a spark plugs with the files and spare chains. Pair a fresh plug with a new <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/replacement-fuel-filter/">fuel filter</a> and a clean air filter for a quick tune-up, or browse more <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/">power equipment parts</a>.',
            1 => 'Store spare plugs in their packaging so the electrodes do not get bent or the gap knocked out of spec while rattling around in a tool bag. Check the gap again before installing a plug that has been carried around for a while.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the difference between the BPMR7A and BPM7A?',
          'a' => 'The R in BPMR7A means it has a built-in resistor. The BPM7A is the non-resistor version. Use the one your engine\'s manual specifies.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What gap should a BPMR7A have?',
          'a' => 'Use the gap listed in your engine\'s operator manual. Check it with a wire gauge before installing.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What thread size is the NGK BPMR7A?',
          'a' => 'It has a 14 mm thread and a 13/16 in (20.8 mm) hex.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How often should I replace it?',
          'a' => 'Follow your manual\'s interval, and replace it any time it is fouled, worn or the engine is hard to start.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/ngk/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/spark-plug-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/spark-plug-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Spark plug',
        'creator' => 'Razor512',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/13144581@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/13144581@N00/2819656917',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/ngk-cmr6h-spark-plug/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/ngk-cmr6h-spark-plug/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/',
      'name' => 'NGK CMR6H Spark Plug',
      'brand' => 'ngk',
      'brand_name' => 'NGK',
      'kind' => 'spark-plug',
      'price' => 9,
      'keyword' => 'ngk cmr6h spark plug',
      'title' => 'NGK CMR6H Spark Plug: Specs, Fitment & Cross Ref',
      'h1' => 'NGK CMR6H Spark Plug',
      'meta' => 'NGK CMR6H spark plug for small handheld engines. Thread and hex size, what the code means, cross reference tips and how to install it the right way.',
      'tagline' => 'A compact 10 mm resistor plug for small handheld power equipment.',
      'summary' => 'The NGK CMR6H spark plug is a compact 10 mm resistor plug used in many small handheld engines, including trimmers, blowers, pole saws and small chainsaws. Its small size suits the tight cylinder heads of lightweight engines.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Compact 10 mm thread with a 5/8 in (16 mm) hex',
        1 => '1/2 in (12.7 mm) reach',
        2 => 'Resistor type',
        3 => 'Heat range 6 in NGK\'s numbering',
        4 => 'Common in small handheld two- and four-stroke engines',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Thread',
          1 => '10 mm',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Hex',
          1 => '5/8 in (16 mm)',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Reach',
          1 => '1/2 in (12.7 mm)',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Resistor',
          1 => 'Yes',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Heat range',
          1 => '6 (NGK)',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What the CMR6H code means',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The cmr6h is part of NGK\'s compact plug family. The C means a 10 mm thread with a 16 mm hex. M marks the compact design. R means it has a resistor. The 6 is the heat range, and H indicates a 1/2 in (12.7 mm) thread reach.',
            1 => 'Compact plugs like the ngk cmr6h are made for small engines where there is not much room in the cylinder head. They are common on trimmers, blowers, edgers, pole saws and small chainsaws. Some small four-stroke handheld engines use them as well.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Finding the right plug',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Always check your operator\'s manual or the plug you are replacing. Thread, reach and heat range all need to match. The CMR6H and the CMR7H look identical but have different heat ranges. In NGK\'s system a higher number is a colder plug, so they are not interchangeable unless your manual lists both.',
            1 => 'Searches for cmr6h cross reference usually come from people trying to match a plug from another brand. Cross-reference charts are a starting point, but heat ranges do not always line up perfectly between brands. When in doubt, use the plug the engine maker specifies.',
            2 => 'If you run several small machines, label which ones use which plug. Crews often run trimmers, blowers and pole saws from different makers, and the plugs look alike. A small chart taped inside the parts bin saves mistakes.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Installing a compact plug',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Small plugs strip threads easily, and many small engines have aluminum heads. Thread the cmr6h spark plug in by hand first. If it does not turn smoothly, back it out and start again. Snug it with the correct 5/8 in plug wrench, following the torque guidance in your manual, and do not over-tighten.',
            1 => 'Check the gap with a wire gauge against your manual before installing. Make sure the plug boot snaps fully onto the terminal. A loose boot causes misfires that feel like a fuel problem. Always replace a plug with the engine cool and the plug wire or boot removed, and never test for spark by holding the plug near the open spark plug hole. Fuel vapor can ignite.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Signs it is time for a new plug',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Hard starting, rough idle, misfires and loss of power are all signs to check the plug. Look at the tip: heavy carbon, oil fouling or a worn electrode all mean it is time to replace it. Because small engines are sensitive, a fresh plug often makes a bigger difference than you would expect.',
            1 => 'Plugs are cheap, so keep a few on hand. If your engine calls for the colder version, see the <a href="/parts/cmr7h-spark-plug/">NGK CMR7H</a>, or browse more <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/">power equipment parts</a>.',
            2 => 'Keep spares in their packaging so the gap stays set and the electrode is protected until you need it.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the thread size of the NGK CMR6H?',
          'a' => 'It has a 10 mm thread, a 5/8 in (16 mm) hex and a 1/2 in (12.7 mm) reach.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a CMR7H instead of a CMR6H?',
          'a' => 'Only if your engine\'s manual lists it. The CMR7H is a colder heat range, so it is not a direct swap in every engine.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is there a cmr6h cross reference for other brands?',
          'a' => 'Other brands make similar plugs, but heat ranges do not always match exactly. Use the plug listed in your manual whenever possible.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/ngk/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/spark-plug-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/spark-plug-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Spark plug',
        'creator' => 'Razor512',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/13144581@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/13144581@N00/2819656917',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/replacement-fuel-filter/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/replacement-fuel-filter/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/',
      'name' => 'Replacement Chainsaw Fuel Filter',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'parts',
      'price' => 8,
      'keyword' => 'chainsaw fuel filter',
      'title' => 'Replacement Chainsaw Fuel Filter: Fitment & Tips',
      'h1' => 'Replacement Chainsaw Fuel Filter',
      'meta' => 'Replacement chainsaw fuel filter for in-tank fuel pickups. How to check fitment, signs of a clogged filter, how to change it and how to prevent fuel trouble.',
      'tagline' => 'In-tank replacement fuel filter to keep debris out of your saw\'s carburetor.',
      'summary' => 'A chainsaw fuel filter sits on the end of the fuel line inside the tank and keeps debris away from the carburetor. This replacement filter is an easy, low-cost part that solves many bogging and stalling problems before they turn into a carburetor rebuild.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'In-tank fuel pickup filter',
        1 => 'Keeps sawdust and grit out of the carburetor',
        2 => 'Low-cost tune-up part',
        3 => 'Check fitment against your fuel line size',
        4 => 'Worth keeping a spare in the saw box',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'In-tank fuel pickup filter',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Chainsaws and small two-stroke equipment',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fitment',
          1 => 'Match to your saw\'s fuel line and manual',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Installation',
          1 => 'Push-on to the end of the fuel line',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Service',
          1 => 'Replace at least once a season',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What a fuel filter does',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Every gas chainsaw pulls fuel through a small filter sitting at the bottom of the tank. Sawdust, grit and bits of debris that get into the tank end up there instead of in the carburetor\'s tiny passages. Over time, that filter loads up and starts to restrict fuel flow.',
            1 => 'A restricted filter is one of the most common reasons a saw runs well at idle but bogs or dies at full throttle. It can also cause a saw to lose power partway through a cut or to stall after a few minutes of work. Before you pull the carburetor apart, put in a new chainsaw fuel filter. It is cheap and often fixes the problem.',
            2 => 'On a busy crew, where saws are refueled in the back of the truck, on the ground or out of a dusty fuel can, filters clog faster than most people expect. Treat the filter as a regular wear part, not something you only think about when the saw quits.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Checking fitment',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Chainsaw fuel filters come in different sizes, with different barb sizes for different fuel line diameters. Check your operator\'s manual or parts list, or compare with the filter you are removing. The filter should push firmly onto the fuel line and stay put. A loose fit can let unfiltered fuel bypass it or let the filter fall off in the tank.',
            1 => 'If you run Husqvarna saws, check whether a model-specific filter is listed. For the T435 top-handle saw, we carry a <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/husqvarna-replacement-fuel-filter-for-t435-chainsaw/">Husqvarna fuel filter for the T435</a>.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Changing the filter',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Work on a cool saw in a clean area. Drain the tank, then use a hooked wire or fuel line tool to pull the line and filter out through the filler opening. Slide the old filter off, push the new one on firmly and drop it back so it rests at the bottom of the tank where it can pick up fuel at any angle.',
            1 => 'Inspect the fuel line while you are there. If it is stiff, cracked or swollen, replace it. A damaged line can let air into the fuel system and cause the same problems as a clogged filter.',
            2 => 'Once the new filter is in, refill with fresh fuel, prime the saw if it has a primer and run it through a few full-throttle cuts to make sure it pulls fuel well at all angles.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Preventing fuel problems',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Keep fuel clean and fresh. Mix in a clean can, use good two-stroke oil and avoid leaving mixed fuel sitting for months. Wipe sawdust away from the fuel cap before opening it. If a saw is going to sit for a while, run it dry or use a stabilized or pre-mixed fuel. Combine a new filter with a fresh spark plug and a clean air filter as part of a regular tune-up, and browse more <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/">chainsaw parts</a> to complete the job.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How often should I change a chainsaw fuel filter?',
          'a' => 'At least once a season for most users, and more often for saws that run daily or in dirty conditions.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will this filter fit my saw?',
          'a' => 'Fuel filters vary by model and fuel line size. Compare it with your old filter or check your operator\'s manual before ordering.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What are the signs of a clogged fuel filter?',
          'a' => 'Bogging or stalling at full throttle, loss of power in the cut and a saw that will not stay running are common signs.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/parts-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/parts-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Route Clearance',
        'creator' => 'Georgia National Guard',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/40994485@N04',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/40994485@N04/33581236171',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/stump-grinding/700-series-greenteeth-pocket-straight/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/stump-grinding/700-series-greenteeth-pocket-straight/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/stump-grinding/',
      'name' => 'Greenteeth 700 Series Pocket (Straight)',
      'brand' => 'green-manufacturing',
      'brand_name' => 'Green Manufacturing',
      'kind' => 'parts',
      'price' => 22,
      'keyword' => 'greenteeth 700 series',
      'title' => 'Greenteeth 700 Series Pocket, Straight',
      'h1' => 'Greenteeth 700 Series Pocket (Straight)',
      'meta' => 'Greenteeth 700 Series straight pocket for stump grinder cutter wheels. What the pocket does, how it fits the system and tips for replacing worn pockets.',
      'tagline' => 'Straight replacement pocket for the Greenteeth 700 Series stump grinder system.',
      'summary' => 'This Greenteeth 700 Series straight pocket is a replacement pocket for cutter wheels set up with the 700 Series system. The pocket holds the tooth on the wheel, so a worn or damaged pocket needs to be replaced to keep the grinder cutting smoothly.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Straight pocket for the Greenteeth 700 Series system',
        1 => 'Holds the tooth securely on the cutter wheel',
        2 => 'Replace worn or damaged pockets to keep the wheel balanced',
        3 => 'Pairs with matching 700 Series teeth and hardware',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'System',
          1 => 'Greenteeth 700 Series',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Style',
          1 => 'Straight pocket',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Stump grinder cutter wheels',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sold as',
          1 => 'Single pocket',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What the pocket does',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'In a pocket-style stump grinder system, the pocket bolts to the cutter wheel and the tooth sits in the pocket. The pocket takes much of the shock and abrasion from the stump and the dirt around it. On a wheel set up with the greenteeth 700 series, straight pockets are used in specific positions in the cutting pattern, alongside left and right pockets.',
            1 => 'Over time, pockets wear from rocks, dirt and the impact of grinding. A worn pocket may not hold the tooth tight, which can let the tooth shift or break. Replacing damaged pockets keeps the teeth secure and the wheel running true.',
            2 => 'Keeping spare pockets on the truck means a damaged pocket does not end a grinding day early. Rocks and buried metal can crack a pocket without warning.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Replacing pockets',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Follow your grinder\'s tooth pattern and the manufacturer\'s instructions when you replace a pocket, and use the correct hardware torqued to spec. Replace pockets in the same position and orientation as the originals. Check all teeth and bolts at the same time. Always lock out the grinder and let the wheel stop completely before working on it, and wear gloves and eye protection. Browse more <a href="/jobsite/stump-grinding/">stump grinding</a> teeth and parts.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a straight pocket for?',
          'a' => 'Straight pockets hold teeth in specific positions in the cutter wheel pattern, working alongside left and right pockets. Follow your wheel\'s pattern when replacing them.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does this fit other Greenteeth series?',
          'a' => 'This pocket is for the 700 Series system. Other series use different pockets and teeth.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'ground',
        1 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/green-manufacturing/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/parts-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/parts-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Skoda Fabia Inlet Manifold',
        'creator' => 'L.C.Nøttaasen',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/35166455@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/35166455@N00/4717400798',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/stump-grinding/green-teeth-allen-head/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/stump-grinding/green-teeth-allen-head/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/stump-grinding/',
      'name' => 'Green Teeth Allen Head Bolt',
      'brand' => 'green-manufacturing',
      'brand_name' => 'Green Manufacturing',
      'kind' => 'parts',
      'price' => 6,
      'keyword' => 'green teeth',
      'title' => 'Green Teeth Allen Head Bolt for Stump Grinder Teeth',
      'h1' => 'Green Teeth Allen Head Bolt',
      'meta' => 'Green Teeth Allen head bolt for mounting stump grinder teeth and pockets. Why hardware matters, inspection tips and how to keep a cutter wheel tight.',
      'tagline' => 'Allen head mounting bolt for Greenteeth stump grinder teeth and pockets.',
      'summary' => 'This Green Teeth Allen head bolt is replacement mounting hardware for the Greenteeth stump grinder tooth system. Teeth and pockets are only as secure as the bolts that hold them, so fresh hardware is a key part of keeping a cutter wheel safe and running smoothly.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Allen head bolt for Greenteeth tooth systems',
        1 => 'Replacement hardware for teeth and pockets',
        2 => 'Socket head design for use with a hex key or bit',
        3 => 'Worth replacing when bolts are worn, stretched or damaged',
        4 => 'Use with matching nuts and torque specs',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'System',
          1 => 'Greenteeth stump grinder teeth',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Head',
          1 => 'Allen (hex socket) head',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Mounting teeth and pockets to the cutter wheel',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sold as',
          1 => 'Single bolt',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fitment',
          1 => 'Match to your tooth series and wheel',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why tooth hardware matters',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Green teeth stump grinder systems are popular because they let you replace individual teeth and pockets instead of a whole wheel. That design depends on the hardware. Every tooth and pocket is held on by bolts that take a beating with every pass through roots, rocks and dirt.',
            1 => 'Worn or stretched bolts can let teeth loosen and shift. A loose tooth cuts poorly, wears out its pocket faster and can break or be thrown from the wheel. Keeping fresh, correct hardware on the wheel is one of the most important parts of stump grinder maintenance.',
            2 => 'Searches for green teeth usually come from operators looking for teeth, pockets or hardware for their wheel. Whatever part you are buying, confirm the series and the position on the wheel before ordering, since teeth, pockets and bolts are not always shared across series.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Allen head design',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'An Allen head bolt uses a hex socket in the head, tightened with a hex key or bit instead of a wrench on the outside of the head. On some cutter wheels, that socket head design makes it easier to reach and tighten the hardware in tight spaces between pockets.',
            1 => 'Over time, the hex socket can fill with dirt or wear from repeated use. Clean the socket before inserting the key so it seats fully. If a socket is rounded out or damaged, replace the bolt instead of trying to make it work. A stripped head becomes a much bigger problem the next time you need to change a tooth.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and replacement',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the wheel at the start of each day and during long grinding jobs. Look for missing, cracked or worn teeth, worn pockets, and bolts that are loose, bent or worn. Use the tooth pattern and torque specifications from Greenteeth or your grinder manufacturer when you replace parts.',
            1 => 'Many operators replace hardware whenever they replace teeth or pockets. It costs little and prevents most loose-tooth problems. Make sure the nuts you use match the bolts and are the type the system calls for.',
            2 => 'Keep a small kit on the grinder or the truck: spare bolts, nuts, the correct hex key or bit and a torque wrench. A wheel can lose a tooth to a hidden rock at any time, and having hardware on hand keeps you working.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Safety on the grinder',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Before you work on the cutter wheel, shut the engine off, remove the key and wait for the wheel to stop completely. Wear gloves and eye protection; teeth are sharp and debris collects around the wheel. When grinding, keep everyone well back from the debris zone, and follow your machine\'s operator manual. See more <a href="/jobsite/stump-grinding/">stump grinding</a> teeth, pockets and hardware, including the <a href="/jobsite/stump-grinding/700-series-greenteeth-pocket-straight/">700 Series straight pocket</a>.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What are Green Teeth?',
          'a' => 'Greenteeth is a stump grinder tooth system made by Green Manufacturing. It uses replaceable teeth, pockets and hardware on the cutter wheel.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why use an Allen head bolt?',
          'a' => 'The hex socket head can be easier to reach and tighten on some cutter wheels. Use the hardware type your tooth system and wheel call for.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How often should I replace tooth bolts?',
          'a' => 'Replace any bolt that is loose, bent, stretched or has a damaged head. Many operators replace hardware each time they change teeth or pockets.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'ground',
        1 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/green-manufacturing/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/parts-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/parts-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Sharpening Chainsaw Blades',
        'creator' => 'NCDOTcommunications',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/39320593@N03',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/39320593@N03/36914543386',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/stump-grinding/standard-stump-grinder-teeth/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/stump-grinding/standard-stump-grinder-teeth/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/stump-grinding/',
      'name' => 'Standard Stump Grinder Teeth',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'parts',
      'price' => 12,
      'keyword' => 'stump cutter teeth',
      'title' => 'Standard Stump Cutter Teeth: Replacement Guide',
      'h1' => 'Standard Stump Cutter Teeth',
      'meta' => 'Standard stump cutter teeth for replacing worn grinder teeth. How to check fitment, when to rotate or replace, and safety tips for working on the wheel.',
      'tagline' => 'Carbide-tipped replacement teeth for standard stump grinder cutter wheels.',
      'summary' => 'These standard stump cutter teeth are carbide-tipped replacements for stump grinders that use standard bolt-on teeth. Fresh, sharp teeth make a grinder cut faster, use less fuel and put less strain on the machine.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Carbide-tipped replacement teeth',
        1 => 'For grinders using standard bolt-on teeth',
        2 => 'Fresh teeth cut faster and reduce machine strain',
        3 => 'Match to your wheel\'s tooth pattern',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Standard bolt-on stump grinder tooth',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Cutting tip',
          1 => 'Carbide',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sold as',
          1 => 'Single tooth',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fitment',
          1 => 'Match to your cutter wheel and pattern',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing replacement teeth',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Stump grinder teeth come in several styles, from standard bolt-on teeth to pocket systems like Greenteeth. These standard teeth are meant for wheels set up for standard teeth. Check your grinder manual or the teeth you are replacing to confirm the style, size and mounting before you order. Stump grinder replacement teeth that do not match the wheel will not cut well and may not mount safely.',
            1 => 'Worn teeth show rounded or chipped carbide, and the grinder slows down and throws dust instead of chips. Some teeth can be rotated to use a fresh edge. When the carbide is gone or cracked, replace the tooth.',
            2 => 'Buying teeth for a stump grinder in sets makes sense for busy crews, since it lets you replace a full row or the whole wheel at once and keep cutting even.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Installing and staying safe',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Shut the engine off, remove the key and wait for the wheel to stop before working on it. Wear gloves and eye protection. Follow the tooth pattern and torque specs for your machine, and replace worn bolts and nuts with the teeth. Check every grinder tooth before each job. When grinding, keep people well back from the debris zone and call in utility locates before you start. Browse more <a href="/jobsite/stump-grinding/">stump grinding</a> teeth and parts.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I know when to replace stump grinder teeth?',
          'a' => 'Look for rounded, chipped or missing carbide and a grinder that slows down or makes fine dust instead of chips.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will these teeth fit my stump grinder?',
          'a' => 'They fit wheels set up for standard teeth. Check your manual or compare with your current teeth before ordering.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'ground',
        1 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/parts-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/parts-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'chainsaw',
        'creator' => 'sf-dvs',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/46207792@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/46207792@N00/64064283',
      ),
    ),
    '/landscaper-tools/corona-22-steel-leaf-rake-with-48-wood-handle/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/landscaper-tools/corona-22-steel-leaf-rake-with-48-wood-handle/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/landscaper-tools/',
      'name' => 'Corona 22 in Steel Leaf Rake with 48 in Wood Handle',
      'brand' => 'corona',
      'brand_name' => 'Corona',
      'kind' => 'jobsite',
      'price' => 30,
      'keyword' => 'corona leaf rake',
      'title' => 'Corona Leaf Rake, 22 in Steel Head, 48 in Handle',
      'h1' => 'Corona Leaf Rake: 22 in Steel Head, 48 in Wood Handle',
      'meta' => 'Corona leaf rake with a 22 in steel head and 48 in wood handle, built for tree job cleanup, leaves and fine debris. Uses, care tips and who it is for.',
      'tagline' => 'A sturdy steel leaf rake for cleaning up after tree work and landscaping.',
      'summary' => 'This Corona leaf rake pairs a 22-inch steel head with a 48-inch wood handle for cleaning up leaves, twigs, sawdust and chip spill on tree and landscape jobs. Steel tines hold up better than plastic when you are raking through gravel, mulch and small sticks.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '22 in steel head for wide, efficient strokes',
        1 => '48 in wood handle with a familiar, solid feel',
        2 => 'Steel tines handle twigs and mixed debris',
        3 => 'Good for lawns, beds and gravel edges',
        4 => 'A simple, durable tool for every cleanup crew',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Head width',
          1 => '22 in',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Head material',
          1 => 'Steel',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Handle',
          1 => '48 in wood',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Leaves, twigs, light debris, cleanup',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Corona',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'The cleanup tool every truck needs',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Tree work leaves a mess. Even after the chipper has eaten the brush and the blower has cleared the driveway, there are always twigs in the lawn, leaves in the beds and small chips around the stump. A good rake is still the fastest way to get that last layer of debris into a pile or onto a tarp. That is the job this Corona leaf rake is built for.',
            1 => 'The 22-inch head is wide enough to cover ground quickly without being so wide that it catches on every shrub and edge. The steel tines flex enough to rake leaves cleanly but stay stiff enough to pull twigs and small sticks out of the grass.',
            2 => 'On removals, a common routine is to rake the lawn into windrows, then pull the windrows onto a tarp and drag it to the chipper or the dump trailer. A wide steel rake makes that routine quick, and it does not mind the occasional chunk of bark or small branch in the pile.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Steel versus plastic',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Plastic and poly rakes are light and gentle on lawns, but they wear quickly when you rake through gravel, rough mulch or the mixed debris that comes off a tree job. Their tines can snap in cold weather. A steel head is more durable and keeps its shape longer, which makes it the better choice for crews that rake every day.',
            1 => 'The trade-off is that a steel rake can be a little harder on delicate turf and a bit heavier. For most crew cleanup work, that durability is worth it.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Handle and handling',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The 48-inch wood handle gives a comfortable, traditional feel. Wood absorbs some vibration and does not get as cold as metal in winter. Keep the handle out of standing water and store the rake under cover so the wood stays sound. A light sanding and a wipe of linseed oil every season will keep the handle smooth and help prevent splinters.',
            1 => 'Check the connection between the head and the handle from time to time. If it loosens, tighten or repair it before the head comes off in the middle of a job.',
            2 => 'Hang the rake by the handle or store it head-up when you are done. Leaving it tines-down on the truck bed bends tines over time, and a rake with bent tines skips over debris instead of collecting it.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Who it is for',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'This rake suits tree crews, landscapers, groundskeepers and homeowners who want a durable, simple tool for leaves and light debris. Pair it with a backpack blower like the <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/blowers-and-other-equipment/husqvarna-580bts-backpack-blower/">Husqvarna 580BTS</a> for big cleanups, and use it to finish the edges, beds and corners the blower misses. Browse more <a href="/jobsite/landscaper-tools/">landscaper tools</a> for the rest of the cleanup kit.',
            1 => 'Wear gloves when raking debris from tree work. Small splinters, thorns and sharp twig ends are common, and gloves make a long cleanup much more comfortable.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How wide is the Corona leaf rake head?',
          'a' => 'The head is 22 in wide, with steel tines.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the handle made of?',
          'a' => 'It has a 48 in wood handle.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is a steel rake good for lawns?',
          'a' => 'Yes, for leaves and twigs. It is more durable than plastic for mixed debris, though plastic rakes are a little gentler on delicate turf.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/corona/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/jobsite-8.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/jobsite-8-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Chipper',
        'creator' => 'photofarmer',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/62528187@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/62528187@N00/6941971832',
      ),
    ),
    '/landscaper-tools/husqvarna-18v-4-gallon-backpack-sprayer/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/landscaper-tools/husqvarna-18v-4-gallon-backpack-sprayer/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/landscaper-tools/',
      'name' => 'Husqvarna 18V 4-Gallon Backpack Sprayer',
      'brand' => 'husqvarna',
      'brand_name' => 'Husqvarna',
      'kind' => 'power-tool',
      'price' => 129,
      'keyword' => 'husqvarna backpack sprayer',
      'title' => 'Husqvarna Backpack Sprayer, 18V 4-Gallon Battery',
      'h1' => 'Husqvarna Backpack Sprayer, 18V 4-Gallon',
      'meta' => 'Husqvarna backpack sprayer with an 18V battery pump and 4-gallon tank. How it works, plant health and landscape uses, and safe application tips.',
      'tagline' => 'Battery-powered 4-gallon backpack sprayer for plant health and landscape work.',
      'summary' => 'The Husqvarna backpack sprayer uses an 18V battery-powered pump and a 4-gallon tank, so you get steady spray pressure without pumping a handle all day. It suits plant health care, landscape maintenance and property work where you cover a lot of ground with a wand.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '18V battery-powered pump, no hand pumping',
        1 => '4-gallon tank for longer runs between refills',
        2 => 'Steady pressure for even coverage',
        3 => 'Backpack design with shoulder straps',
        4 => 'Suited to plant health care and landscape spraying',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Tank capacity',
          1 => '4 gallons',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Power',
          1 => '18V battery-powered pump',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Carry',
          1 => 'Backpack with shoulder straps',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Liquid applications for landscape and plant health',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Husqvarna',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a battery backpack sprayer',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Manual backpack sprayers work, but they ask a lot. One hand pumps while the other aims the wand, and pressure rises and falls with every stroke. A battery-powered pump changes that. Pull the trigger and the pump keeps pressure steady, so coverage is more even and your arm is free to focus on aiming.',
            1 => 'That matters on plant health jobs where you treat multiple beds, shrubs or small trees on a property. A backpack sprayer 4 gallon in size holds enough to make real progress before you need to refill, while still being light enough to carry once it is full.',
            2 => 'It also helps with consistency. Steady pressure means a more consistent droplet size and application rate, which matters when you are following a label rate or calibrating a sprayer for a specific product.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Plant health and landscape uses',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Arborists and landscapers use backpack sprayers for a range of applications: foliar fertilizers, horticultural oils, herbicide spot treatments around landscape beds, and other liquid products labeled for the target plant and pest. The 4-gallon tank and backpack design make it practical for walking a property and treating plants as you go.',
            1 => 'Clean the tank, pump and wand thoroughly after every use, especially when switching products. Residue from an herbicide can damage the next plant you spray. Triple-rinse according to the product label and dispose of rinse water properly.',
            2 => 'Calibrate before you start a job. Spray a measured area with plain water, time it and check how much you used. That tells you how to mix and how fast to walk so you apply the rate on the label. Recheck calibration when you change nozzles or products.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Safe and legal application',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The pesticide label is the law. Read and follow it for every product you mix and apply, including the personal protective equipment it requires, mixing rates, re-entry intervals and environmental restrictions. Many states require a pesticide applicator license for commercial application. Check your state\'s rules before you treat customers\' trees and landscapes.',
            1 => 'Wear the PPE the label calls for, which often includes chemical-resistant gloves, eye protection and long sleeves. Avoid spraying in windy conditions, and keep sprays away from water, pollinators and non-target plants. Store products and the sprayer securely away from children and pets.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Battery care and who it is for',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Charge the battery before each job and store it in a cool, dry place. Do not leave it in a hot truck cab for long periods. Check your battery and charger compatibility before you buy, since the sprayer needs a compatible 18V pack.',
            1 => 'The Husqvarna 4 gallon backpack sprayer suits plant health technicians, landscapers and property owners who spray regularly and want to stop hand pumping. Browse more <a href="/jobsite/landscaper-tools/">landscaper tools</a> for the rest of your maintenance kit.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How big is the tank on the Husqvarna backpack sprayer?',
          'a' => 'It holds 4 gallons.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does it need to be pumped by hand?',
          'a' => 'No. An 18V battery runs the pump, so you get steady pressure without hand pumping.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I need a license to spray for customers?',
          'a' => 'Many states require a pesticide applicator license for commercial application. Check your state\'s requirements and always follow the product label.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
        1 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/husqvarna/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/power-tool-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/power-tool-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Trimming grass and weeds in a lush garden with a string trimmer',
        'creator' => 'nenad53',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/202780880@N02',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/202780880@N02/54574042635',
      ),
    ),
    '/landscaper-tools/husqvarna-trim-force-square-trimmer-line/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/landscaper-tools/husqvarna-trim-force-square-trimmer-line/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/landscaper-tools/',
      'name' => 'Husqvarna Trim Force Square Trimmer Line',
      'brand' => 'husqvarna',
      'brand_name' => 'Husqvarna',
      'kind' => 'parts',
      'price' => 20,
      'keyword' => 'trim line',
      'title' => 'Husqvarna Trim Force Square Trim Line',
      'h1' => 'Husqvarna Trim Force Square Trim Line',
      'meta' => 'Husqvarna Trim Force square trim line for string trimmers. Why a square profile cuts cleaner, how to pick a diameter and how to load and store line.',
      'tagline' => 'Square-profile string trimmer line for clean cuts in grass and weeds.',
      'summary' => 'Husqvarna Trim Force square trim line is a square-profile string trimmer line made for cutting through grass and weeds cleanly. The square edges bite into stems better than round line, which helps on thick growth along fences, beds and tree bases.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Square profile with cutting edges',
        1 => 'Cleaner cuts in thick grass and weeds',
        2 => 'Fits trimmer heads that accept the matching diameter',
        3 => 'Good for edging around beds, fences and hardscape',
        4 => 'Husqvarna line for Husqvarna and other trimmers',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Profile',
          1 => 'Square',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'String trimmers and brushcutters with line heads',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => 'Match to your trimmer head (see listing)',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Husqvarna',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'Nylon-based trimmer line',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a square profile',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Round trimmer line is durable and cheap, but it tends to smash and tear grass rather than slice it. Square trim line has four edges that act like small blades as the head spins. The result is a cleaner cut, especially in tough weeds and thick grass, and a neater finish along edges.',
            1 => 'For tree crews and landscapers, that matters around the base of trees, along fence lines and in overgrown corners where round line struggles. Square line can wear a little faster on concrete and stone edges, so pick the right line for the job.',
            2 => 'Many landscape crews keep both on the truck: square line for lawns and overgrown areas, round line for heavy edging along hardscape. Matching the line to the job saves time and line.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Picking a diameter',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Husqvarna string trimmer line comes in different diameters, and the right one depends on your trimmer head and engine. Thicker line cuts heavier growth and lasts longer, but it needs more power to spin. Too thick a line on a small trimmer makes the engine bog and wears out the clutch.',
            1 => 'Check your trimmer head or operator\'s manual for the recommended diameter range. If you run a mix of light and heavy trimmers, keep a spool of each size on the truck and label them.',
            2 => 'Buying line in larger spools saves money for crews that trim every day, while smaller packs make sense for homeowners. Either way, the same rules apply: the right diameter for your head, wound neatly and stored properly.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Loading and storing line',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Wind line evenly onto the spool in the direction marked on the head, keeping it tight and not crossing the two sides on dual-line heads. Leave the right length out of the eyelets and trim excess before starting. Uneven winding causes line to jam and stop feeding.',
            1 => 'Store trimmer line out of direct sun and away from heat. Line that dries out becomes brittle and breaks more easily. Some users soak line in water before loading to help keep it flexible. Keep spools in a sealed bag or bin to stop them drying out over the season.',
            2 => 'If your trimmer has a bump-feed head, keep the bump knob and eyelets clean and replace worn eyelets. Worn eyelets cut line and cause it to break off short.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Safe trimming',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Wear eye protection, hearing protection and long pants. Trimmers throw debris hard and fast. Keep bystanders and pets well back, and be careful around tree trunks: repeated trimmer contact damages bark and can seriously injure young trees. A mulch ring around tree bases cuts down on trimming close to the trunk.',
            1 => 'Browse more <a href="/jobsite/landscaper-tools/">landscaper tools</a> for the rest of your trimming and cleanup kit.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is square trim line better than round?',
          'a' => 'Square line usually cuts cleaner in thick grass and weeds. Round line can be more durable along concrete and stone edges.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What diameter trim line do I need?',
          'a' => 'Check your trimmer head or manual. Use the diameter range it recommends for your engine and head.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will Husqvarna trim line fit other trimmers?',
          'a' => 'Usually, as long as the diameter matches what your trimmer head accepts.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'ground',
        1 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/husqvarna/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/parts-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/parts-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Route Clearance',
        'creator' => 'Georgia National Guard',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/40994485@N04',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/40994485@N04/33581236171',
      ),
    ),
    '/lowering-devices/jail-brake-lowering-device/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/lowering-devices/jail-brake-lowering-device/',
      'category' => '/rigging/lowering-devices/',
      'name' => 'Jail Brake Lowering Device',
      'brand' => 'jail-brake',
      'brand_name' => 'Jail Brake',
      'kind' => 'rigging-device',
      'price' => 349,
      'keyword' => 'jailbreak rigging device',
      'title' => 'Jail Brake Lowering Device: Jailbreak Rigging Device',
      'h1' => 'Jail Brake Lowering Device (Jailbreak Rigging Device)',
      'meta' => 'Jail Brake lowering device, the jailbreak rigging device for controlled lowering in tree removals. How friction lowering works and safe rigging tips.',
      'tagline' => 'Tree-mounted friction device for controlled lowering on rigging jobs.',
      'summary' => 'The Jail Brake lowering device, often searched as the jailbreak rigging device, is a friction device that mounts to the tree to control the lowering line on removals. It lets the ground crew add or remove friction to lower heavy pieces smoothly instead of relying on wraps around the trunk.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Friction lowering device for tree rigging',
        1 => 'Mounts to the tree to control the lowering line',
        2 => 'Helps the ground crew lower heavy pieces smoothly',
        3 => 'Reduces wear on the trunk compared with wraps around the stem',
        4 => 'For trained rigging crews only',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Friction lowering device',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Mounting',
          1 => 'Attached to the tree',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Controlled lowering in removals and rigging',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rope',
          1 => 'Use the rope types and sizes the manufacturer specifies',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Working load',
          1 => 'See manufacturer markings and instructions',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What a lowering device does',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'When you rig a piece out of a tree, the energy of that piece has to go somewhere. The rigging block or natural crotch redirects the line, and the lowering device at the base turns that energy into heat through friction. The ground worker controls how much friction is applied and lets the piece down at a controlled speed.',
            1 => 'Without a lowering device, crews take wraps around the trunk. That works on light pieces, but it is hard to control on heavy ones, it damages bark and it is difficult to adjust mid-lower. A dedicated device like the Jail Brake gives the ground crew a consistent, adjustable friction point.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How it fits into a rigging system',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A typical system has a rigging point aloft, often a block on a sling, a rigging line tied to the piece, and the lowering device mounted to the tree at the base. The ground worker manages the tail of the line through the device. More wraps or more friction slow the piece; fewer let it run faster.',
            1 => 'Search terms like jailbreak lowering device usually come from crews comparing it to other friction devices such as port-a-wrap style tools and bollards. The idea is the same: give the ground worker control. Fit, mounting and rope compatibility vary by device, so follow the manufacturer\'s instructions.',
            2 => 'Mounting matters. The device needs to be attached securely at a height and position that lets the ground worker stand clear of the drop zone, keep a good grip on the tail and see the piece coming down. Plan the setup before the climber makes the first cut, not after.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Rigging forces and safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Rigging forces can be much higher than the weight of the piece, especially when a piece is allowed to free-fall before the line catches it. Every component in the system, including the lowering device, slings, blocks, rope and the tree itself, must be rated for the loads and in good condition. Do not exceed the device\'s rated working load.',
            1 => 'Rigging should only be planned and performed by trained crews following ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions. Inspect the device before every use for cracks, deformation, grooving and wear. Keep the ground crew out of the drop zone and use clear communication between the climber and the person on the lowering device.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Who should use it',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The Jail Brake suits removal crews that rig regularly and want a dedicated friction device at the base. Pair it with a good rigging block, rigging slings and a rigging line that are all rated for the loads you plan to put on them. Browse more <a href="/rigging/lowering-devices/">lowering devices</a> to compare options.',
            1 => 'If you are new to rigging with a mounted friction device, start with light pieces, practice with the full crew and build up gradually. Learn how many wraps or how much friction a given piece needs before you take on big wood.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the jailbreak rigging device used for?',
          'a' => 'It is a friction lowering device mounted to the tree that lets the ground crew control the speed of rigged pieces during removals.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How is it different from taking wraps on the trunk?',
          'a' => 'A lowering device gives more consistent, adjustable friction and avoids damaging bark, especially with heavier pieces.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Who should use a lowering device?',
          'a' => 'Trained rigging crews following ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions. Rigging forces can be very high.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-5.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-5-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Nautical #5',
        'creator' => 'Graham B Finney',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/188487900@N02',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/188487900@N02/51798984894',
      ),
    ),
    '/micro-pulleys/dmm-hitch-climber-pulley-2/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/micro-pulleys/dmm-hitch-climber-pulley-2/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/micro-pulleys/',
      'name' => 'DMM Hitch Climber Pulley',
      'brand' => 'dmm',
      'brand_name' => 'DMM',
      'kind' => 'pulley',
      'price' => 65,
      'keyword' => 'climber pulley',
      'title' => 'DMM Hitch Climber Pulley for Arborist Climbing',
      'h1' => 'DMM Hitch Climber Pulley',
      'meta' => 'DMM Hitch Climber pulley for arborist friction hitch systems. How it tends the hitch, how to set it up, and inspection and safety notes for climbers.',
      'tagline' => 'A climber pulley built to tend a friction hitch on a doubled-rope system.',
      'summary' => 'The DMM Hitch Climber pulley is a climber pulley designed to sit below a friction hitch and tend it as you advance. It became one of the standard pieces in arborist moving-rope systems because it makes slack tending smoother and the hitch easier to manage.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Designed to tend friction hitches in moving-rope systems',
        1 => 'Attachment points for hitch, carabiner and hitch cord',
        2 => 'Smooth sheave for easy slack tending',
        3 => 'Keeps the hitch aligned and the bridge connection clean',
        4 => 'A long-standing standard in arborist climbing kits',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Hitch-tending climber pulley',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'DMM',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Body',
          1 => 'Aluminum side plates',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Attachment',
          1 => 'Multiple attachment holes for hitch and carabiner',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rope',
          1 => 'Arborist climbing ropes, check DMM\'s diameter range',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Standards',
          1 => 'See DMM markings and instructions',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What the Hitch Climber does',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'In a moving-rope system, the climber advances by pulling slack through a friction hitch. The hitch climber pulley sits right below the hitch on the rope. As you pull the rope\'s tail, the pulley pushes the hitch up, so the system tends slack smoothly without you having to reach up and manage the hitch by hand.',
            1 => 'That is why it caught on. Before pulleys like this, climbers often tended hitches by hand or used a simple ring setup. The Hitch Climber made climbing smoother and faster and kept the hitch dressed and aligned through the day.',
            2 => 'It also helps keep the friction hitch from rolling or getting pulled out of shape, which makes descents more predictable. Many climbers find that their hitch grips and releases more consistently with a hitch-tending pulley in place.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How it is set up',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The pulley has multiple attachment holes. A typical setup attaches the friction hitch cord and the bridge carabiner through the pulley, with the rope running over the sheave. Exact setups vary, and the friction hitch you use, such as a Distel or VT, affects how the system behaves.',
            1 => 'Learn the setup from a qualified trainer and follow DMM\'s instructions. A small mistake in how the hitch cord is attached or how the carabiner is loaded can turn a reliable system into a dangerous one. Practice on the ground at low height before you climb with a new system.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing a climber pulley',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The DMM hitch climber pulley is popular because it is well made and easy to use, but it is not the only option. Some climbers prefer different pulley shapes, swivels or lighter designs. Whatever you choose, make sure it is rated for life support, compatible with your rope diameter and designed for the way you climb.',
            1 => 'If you are just starting to use a hitch climber, buy a complete system that has been tested together: rope, hitch cord, pulley and carabiners. Compatibility between parts matters more than any single component.',
            2 => 'Weight, sheave size and how the pulley sits on your bridge all affect how a system feels. Try a few setups with a trainer or experienced climber before settling on one.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Life-support gear must meet the applicable standards, be used according to the manufacturer\'s instructions and ANSI Z133, and be inspected before every use. Only trained climbers should use it.',
            1 => 'Inspect the pulley before every climb: check the side plates for cracks, burrs or deformation, make sure the sheave spins freely, and look for grooving where the rope runs. Retire it if it has been dropped from height onto a hard surface, shows damage or does not run smoothly. Browse more <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/micro-pulleys/">micro pulleys</a> to compare options.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a hitch climber pulley?',
          'a' => 'It is a pulley that sits below the friction hitch in a moving-rope system and pushes the hitch up as you pull slack, making climbing smoother.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use any friction hitch with it?',
          'a' => 'Common arborist hitches such as the Distel and VT are often used. Follow DMM\'s instructions and get training on the setup you use.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I inspect the DMM Hitch Climber?',
          'a' => 'Check for cracks, burrs, deformation and grooving, and make sure the sheave spins freely. Retire it if it is damaged or has been dropped onto a hard surface from height.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/dmm/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/pulley-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/pulley-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Block and Tackle',
        'creator' => 'www.mgaylard.co.uk and thanks for looking',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/16564965@N04',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/16564965@N04/52721499095',
      ),
    ),
    '/other-gear/soft-canopy-anchor/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/other-gear/soft-canopy-anchor/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/other-gear/',
      'name' => 'Soft Canopy Anchor',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'price' => 69,
      'keyword' => 'canopy anchors',
      'title' => 'Canopy Anchors: Soft Textile Anchor for Climbers',
      'h1' => 'Soft Canopy Anchor',
      'meta' => 'Soft canopy anchors for SRT and moving-rope tree climbing. How they work, how to choose one and the inspection and safety basics every climber needs.',
      'tagline' => 'A textile canopy anchor for setting a secure tie-in point aloft.',
      'summary' => 'Canopy anchors give climbers a secure tie-in point around a stem or limb in the canopy, and this soft canopy anchor does it with textile instead of steel hardware. It is light, wraps around a range of stem sizes and helps protect both the rope and the tree\'s bark.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Textile construction for a light, compact canopy anchor',
        1 => 'Wraps around a range of stem and limb sizes',
        2 => 'Protects bark and rope compared with a bare-rope tie-in',
        3 => 'Works with SRT and moving-rope systems when used as instructed',
        4 => 'Easy to pack in a climbing kit',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Textile canopy anchor',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Tie-in point in the canopy',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Systems',
          1 => 'SRT and moving-rope, per instructions',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Retrieval',
          1 => 'Check the listing for retrievable configuration',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rating',
          1 => 'See the manufacturer\'s markings and instructions',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why use a canopy anchor',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Tying in directly around a limb works, but it puts friction on the bark and the rope and does not always give you the best rope angle. Canopy anchors give a clean, consistent anchor point. They spread the load around the stem, protect the tree and reduce rope wear.',
            1 => 'A soft canopy anchor uses a textile sling or cord instead of a metal anchor. That makes it lighter and quieter in the kit bag, and it conforms well to irregular stems. Many climbers prefer textile around the tree for that reason.',
            2 => 'Canopy anchors are especially common with SRT, where the rope is fixed in the tree and the climber ascends with mechanical devices. A good anchor makes it easier to set a reliable tie-in and to choose the best spot for the work you plan to do.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'SRT and moving-rope use',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'In single rope technique, the canopy anchor is where your rope is fixed in the tree. In moving-rope systems, it can provide a friction-saving redirect at the tie-in point. The details depend on the anchor\'s design, your system and the manufacturer\'s instructions.',
            1 => 'Whatever system you use, think about the forces at the anchor. In SRT, the full load goes onto a single anchor point and the limb or stem must be strong enough. Inspect the tree carefully, choose a sound, well-attached stem and avoid dead or weak wood.',
            2 => 'If you are setting a canopy anchor for SRT, consider whether the tie-in point allows a good work position for the whole job, or whether you will need to re-anchor partway through. Thinking it through from the ground saves time and reduces unnecessary changeovers aloft.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing a canopy anchor',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Look at the anchor\'s length and how it fits the stem sizes you usually climb on, the hardware it uses, whether it can be set and retrieved from the ground and whether it is rated for life support. Some climbers keep two anchors of different lengths for different trees.',
            1 => 'Make sure every connection is compatible: rope, carabiners, rings and the anchor itself. A canopy anchor is only as strong as the weakest link in the system.',
            2 => 'Practice setting and retrieving your anchor on the ground before you rely on it in the tree. Know how it should look when properly set, so you can spot a problem from below.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Life-support gear must meet the applicable standards, be used according to the manufacturer\'s instructions and ANSI Z133, and be inspected before every use. Only trained climbers should use it. Textiles wear faster than metal, so inspect the anchor for cuts, abrasion, glazing, chemical contamination and UV damage before every climb. Retire it if you have any doubt.',
            1 => 'Keep textiles away from chainsaw fuel, bar oil and sharp tools. Store them dry and out of sunlight. Browse more <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/other-gear/">climbing gear</a> to complete your kit.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What are canopy anchors used for?',
          'a' => 'They create a secure tie-in point around a stem or limb in the canopy, protecting the tree and rope and giving a consistent anchor.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is a soft canopy anchor safe for SRT?',
          'a' => 'It can be when it is rated for life support, used according to the manufacturer\'s instructions and set on a sound stem. Get training in SRT anchoring first.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How often should I inspect a textile anchor?',
          'a' => 'Before every climb. Look for cuts, abrasion, glazing, contamination and UV damage, and retire it if in doubt.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Tree Pruning in RVA (Carytown, Virginia)',
        'creator' => 'Gamma Man',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06/48982146938',
      ),
    ),
    '/parts-and-accessories/husqvarna-chainsaw-maintenance-kit-for-445-and-450/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/parts-and-accessories/husqvarna-chainsaw-maintenance-kit-for-445-and-450/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/',
      'name' => 'Husqvarna Chainsaw Maintenance Kit for 445 and 450',
      'brand' => 'husqvarna',
      'brand_name' => 'Husqvarna',
      'kind' => 'parts',
      'price' => 25,
      'keyword' => 'husqvarna chainsaw tune-up kit',
      'title' => 'Husqvarna Chainsaw Tune-Up Kit for 445 and 450',
      'h1' => 'Husqvarna Chainsaw Tune-Up Kit for 445 and 450',
      'meta' => 'Husqvarna chainsaw tune-up kit for the 445 and 450. What a maintenance kit covers, when to use it, and how to tune up your saw for easy starts.',
      'tagline' => 'A tune-up kit with the wear parts a 445 or 450 needs each season.',
      'summary' => 'This Husqvarna chainsaw tune-up kit bundles common wear parts for the Husqvarna 445 and 450 saws, so you can service your saw in one go. Fresh filters and a new spark plug fix a lot of hard-starting and low-power problems.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Made for Husqvarna 445 and 450 chainsaws',
        1 => 'Bundles common wear parts for a seasonal tune-up',
        2 => 'Saves time compared with buying parts one by one',
        3 => 'Helps prevent hard starting and power loss',
        4 => 'Check the listing for exact contents',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fits',
          1 => 'Husqvarna 445 and 450',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Maintenance and tune-up kit',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Husqvarna',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Contents',
          1 => 'Common wear parts, see listing',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Service',
          1 => 'Once a season or per manual',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a tune-up kit',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Chainsaws do not usually fail all at once. They get harder to start, lose a bit of power, smoke a bit more and start to stall. Most of the time, the cause is a set of cheap wear parts: a dirty air filter, a clogged fuel filter or a tired spark plug. A Husqvarna maintenance kit puts those parts together in one package for your saw.',
            1 => 'The 445 and 450 are popular saws with landowners and part-time cutters, and many of them get used hard during storm season and firewood time, then sit for months. A once-a-season tune-up keeps them ready for the next time you need them.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What to do with the kit',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Start with a cool saw on a clean bench. Clean the outside of the saw, especially around the air filter cover and the fuel cap. Replace the air filter, remove and replace the spark plug, gapped to the figure in your manual, and fish out the fuel filter from the tank to replace it. Check the fuel line for cracks while you are there.',
            1 => 'Then look at the rest of the saw. Clean the cooling fins and the starter area, check the bar rails, sprocket and chain, and make sure the chain brake works. Fill with fresh fuel and bar oil and run it through a few cuts.',
            2 => 'Keep a simple log of when you did each tune-up. A strip of tape inside the saw case with the date is enough. It is easy to lose track of when you last changed filters, especially if the saw only runs a few weeks a year.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Signs your saw needs a tune-up',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Hard starting, rough idle, bogging under load, loss of power or excessive smoke are all reasons to service the saw. Many people search for a husqvarna chainsaw tune-up kit after a saw that ran well last fall will not start this spring. Old fuel is often part of the problem, so drain old fuel before you start.',
            1 => 'If a new plug, filters and fresh fuel do not fix it, the saw may need carburetor or other service. Take it to a qualified shop.',
            2 => 'Using fresh fuel and running the carburetor dry before storage prevents most of these problems in the first place. Pre-mixed fuel is a good choice for saws that sit for long stretches between uses.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Matching the kit to your saw',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'This kit is listed for the 445 and 450. Other Husqvarna models use different parts, so check your model number before ordering. If you run a <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-450-rancher/">Husqvarna 450 Rancher</a>, this kit is made for your saw. For other models, browse <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/">chainsaw parts</a> for individual filters and plugs.',
            1 => 'Wear gloves when handling the chain and work in a ventilated area away from flames when draining fuel.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Which saws does this tune-up kit fit?',
          'a' => 'It is listed for the Husqvarna 445 and 450 chainsaws.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What does the kit include?',
          'a' => 'It bundles common wear parts for a tune-up. Check the listing for the exact contents before ordering.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How often should I tune up my chainsaw?',
          'a' => 'At least once a season, or more often for saws that run regularly. Follow your manual\'s service schedule.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/husqvarna/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/parts-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/parts-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Skoda Fabia Inlet Manifold',
        'creator' => 'L.C.Nøttaasen',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/35166455@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/35166455@N00/4717400798',
      ),
    ),
    '/parts/bpmr8y-spark-plug/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/parts/bpmr8y-spark-plug/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/',
      'name' => 'NGK BPMR8Y Spark Plug',
      'brand' => 'ngk',
      'brand_name' => 'NGK',
      'kind' => 'spark-plug',
      'price' => 5,
      'keyword' => 'bpmr8y',
      'title' => 'NGK BPMR8Y Spark Plug: Specs, Cross Reference & Tips',
      'h1' => 'NGK BPMR8Y Spark Plug',
      'meta' => 'NGK BPMR8Y, the resistor version of the NGK BPM8Y spark plug. Thread and hex size, heat range, cross reference tips and how to install it properly.',
      'tagline' => 'A 14 mm resistor plug in a colder heat range for chainsaws and small engines.',
      'summary' => 'The NGK BPMR8Y is a 14 mm resistor spark plug with a projected tip and a heat range of 8, and it is the resistor version of the NGK BPM8Y spark plug. It is used in a range of chainsaws and small two-stroke engines that call for a slightly colder plug than the BPMR7A.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '14 mm thread, 13/16 in (20.8 mm) hex',
        1 => 'Resistor type, the R version of the BPM8Y',
        2 => 'Heat range 8, one step colder than a 7',
        3 => 'Projected insulator tip',
        4 => 'Common in chainsaws and small two-stroke engines',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Thread',
          1 => '14 mm',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Hex',
          1 => '13/16 in (20.8 mm)',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Reach',
          1 => '3/8 in (9.5 mm)',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Resistor',
          1 => 'Yes',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Heat range',
          1 => '8 (NGK)',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Tip',
          1 => 'Projected insulator',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'BPMR8Y versus BPM8Y',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The only letter that separates the bpmr8y from the BPM8Y is the R, which stands for resistor. A resistor plug reduces electrical noise from the ignition system. Many modern engines with electronic ignition specify a resistor plug, and some older engines were designed around the non-resistor version.',
            1 => 'If your manual lists the BPMR8Y, use it. If it lists the BPM8Y, check whether the resistor version is an approved substitute before swapping. In practice, many people looking for an ngk bpmr8y spark plug are replacing a BPM8Y and want to know whether it will work. The answer depends on your engine\'s ignition, so the manual is the final word.',
            2 => 'The BPMR8Y is found in a range of chainsaws and other small two-stroke engines. Because several brands of equipment use it, it is a plug many shops keep in stock, and it is worth keeping a couple in the truck if your saws call for it.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Heat range and fitment',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'In NGK\'s numbering, a higher number means a colder plug. An 8 runs cooler than a 7, which can suit engines that run hot or at high load. The wrong heat range causes problems: too hot can lead to pre-ignition and engine damage, too cold can lead to fouling.',
            1 => 'Searches for bpmr8y cross reference usually come from people matching other brands\' plugs. Cross-reference charts are helpful, but heat ranges do not always map exactly between brands. Use the plug the engine maker specifies, or ask a trusted dealer.',
            2 => 'Do not change heat range to fix a running problem. If a plug keeps fouling or looks overheated, the cause is usually fuel mix, carburetor tuning, an air leak or a dirty filter. Fix the cause and keep the specified plug.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Installing it',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the gap with a wire gauge against your manual before installing. Thread the plug in by hand to avoid cross-threading, then snug it with a plug wrench. Do not over-tighten, especially in aluminum heads. Push the plug boot on until it clicks or seats fully. Always replace a plug with the engine cool and the plug wire or boot removed, and never test for spark by holding the plug near the open spark plug hole. Fuel vapor can ignite.',
            1 => 'Read the old plug when it comes out. A dry, light tan or gray color is normal. Wet, black or oily deposits suggest a rich mixture, oil problem or dirty air filter. White or blistered electrodes suggest a lean condition that needs attention.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Keep a few on hand',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Spark plugs are cheap and easy to swap, and a fresh plug fixes a lot of starting problems. Keep spares in their packaging in the saw box along with spare filters. If your engine uses the hotter 7 heat range, see the <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/ngk-bpmr7a-spark-plug/">NGK BPMR7A</a>. For other parts, browse <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/">power equipment parts</a>.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a BPMR8Y instead of a BPM8Y?',
          'a' => 'Often, but not always. The BPMR8Y adds a resistor. Check your engine\'s manual or ask a dealer before swapping.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the heat range of the BPMR8Y?',
          'a' => 'It is an 8 on NGK\'s scale, one step colder than a 7.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What thread size is the BPMR8Y?',
          'a' => 'It has a 14 mm thread and a 13/16 in (20.8 mm) hex.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/ngk/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/spark-plug-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/spark-plug-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Spark plug',
        'creator' => 'Razor512',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/13144581@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/13144581@N00/2819656917',
      ),
    ),
    '/parts/cmr7h-spark-plug/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/parts/cmr7h-spark-plug/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/',
      'name' => 'NGK CMR7H Spark Plug',
      'brand' => 'ngk',
      'brand_name' => 'NGK',
      'kind' => 'spark-plug',
      'price' => 9,
      'keyword' => 'ngk cmr7h spark plug',
      'title' => 'NGK CMR7H Spark Plug: Specs, Fitment & Tips',
      'h1' => 'NGK CMR7H Spark Plug',
      'meta' => 'NGK CMR7H spark plug for small handheld engines. Thread, hex and reach, heat range, how it compares with the CMR6H and how to install it properly.',
      'tagline' => 'A compact 10 mm resistor plug in a colder heat range for small engines.',
      'summary' => 'The NGK CMR7H spark plug is a compact 10 mm resistor plug used in many small handheld engines, including trimmers, blowers, pole saws and small chainsaws. It shares its size with the CMR6H but runs one step colder.',
      'highlights' => 
