          1 => '7 (NGK)',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Reading the CMR7H code',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The ngk cmr7h follows NGK\'s compact plug naming. C is a 10 mm thread with a 16 mm hex. M marks the compact design for small engines. R means resistor. The 7 is the heat range, and H indicates a 1/2 in (12.7 mm) reach.',
            1 => 'Compact plugs are made for small cylinder heads where a full-size plug will not fit. The cmr7h spark plug shows up in trimmers, blowers, edgers, pole saws and small chainsaws, as well as some small four-stroke handheld engines.',
            2 => 'Plenty of crews run several brands of small engines, so the same plug may turn up in a trimmer, a blower and a pole saw on the same truck. That makes the CMR7H one of the handier plugs to keep stocked, as long as each machine actually calls for it.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'CMR7H versus CMR6H',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'These two plugs look the same and fit the same holes, but they are different heat ranges. In NGK\'s system, a higher number means a colder plug. The CMR7H is one step colder than the CMR6H. Engines that run hotter or at sustained high load may call for the colder plug.',
            1 => 'Only swap between them if your manual lists both. A plug that is too hot for the engine can cause pre-ignition and engine damage, while a plug that is too cold can foul. If you are not sure, match the plug that came out of the engine and confirm it against the manual. See the <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/ngk-cmr6h-spark-plug/">NGK CMR6H</a> for the hotter version.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Installing a compact plug',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Small threads strip easily, especially in aluminum heads. Thread the plug in by hand first and only use the 5/8 in plug wrench for the final snugging. Check the gap with a wire gauge against your manual, and make sure the boot is seated fully on the terminal. Always replace a plug with the engine cool and the plug wire or boot removed, and never test for spark by holding the plug near the open spark plug hole. Fuel vapor can ignite.',
            1 => 'When you pull the old plug, look at its color and deposits. Heavy black carbon can mean a rich mixture or dirty air filter. Oily deposits can point to too much oil in the mix. A light tan or gray tip usually means the engine is running well.',
            2 => 'Keep spare plugs in their packaging until you use them, so the gap stays where it was set and the electrode does not get damaged in a tool bag.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'When to replace it',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Replace the plug at the interval in your manual, or any time the engine is hard to start, idles roughly or loses power. A worn or fouled plug is cheap to replace and is one of the first things to check. Keep a few spares in the truck with your other <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/">power equipment parts</a>.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the difference between the CMR7H and CMR6H?',
          'a' => 'They are the same size, but the CMR7H is one heat range colder. Use the one your engine\'s manual specifies.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What thread size is the NGK CMR7H?',
          'a' => 'It has a 10 mm thread, a 5/8 in (16 mm) hex and a 1/2 in (12.7 mm) reach.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the CMR7H a resistor plug?',
          'a' => 'Yes. The R in the code means it has a built-in resistor.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/ngk/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/spark-plug-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/spark-plug-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Spark plug',
        'creator' => 'Razor512',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/13144581@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/13144581@N00/2819656917',
      ),
    ),
    '/parts/husqvarna-vari-cut-s45-diamond-wheel/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/parts/husqvarna-vari-cut-s45-diamond-wheel/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/',
      'name' => 'Husqvarna Vari-Cut S45 Diamond Wheel',
      'brand' => 'husqvarna',
      'brand_name' => 'Husqvarna',
      'kind' => 'parts',
      'price' => 119,
      'keyword' => 'husqvarna vari-cut s45',
      'title' => 'Husqvarna Vari-Cut S45 Diamond Wheel: Buying Guide',
      'h1' => 'Husqvarna Vari-Cut S45 Diamond Wheel',
      'meta' => 'Husqvarna Vari-Cut S45 diamond wheel for power cutters. What it cuts, how to match size and arbor, and safety tips for dust control and blade care.',
      'tagline' => 'A general-purpose diamond blade for power cutters on jobsite cutting work.',
      'summary' => 'The Husqvarna Vari-Cut S45 is a general-purpose diamond wheel for power cutters, used for cutting concrete, masonry and other building materials on the jobsite. It is a practical blade for crews who need to make occasional cuts in hardscape around tree and landscape work.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Segmented diamond blade for power cutters',
        1 => 'General-purpose cutting in concrete and masonry',
        2 => 'Part of Husqvarna\'s Vari-Cut blade range',
        3 => 'A practical blade for occasional jobsite cutting',
        4 => 'Check the listing for diameter and arbor size',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Segmented diamond blade',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Range',
          1 => 'Husqvarna Vari-Cut, S45 level',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Concrete, masonry and building materials',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Machine',
          1 => 'Power cutters and saws rated for the blade',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Size',
          1 => 'See listing for diameter and arbor',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a tree crew might need one',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Tree and landscape work does not always stop at the soil line. Crews sometimes need to cut out a section of concrete or pavers to reach roots, trim a curb that a root has lifted, or cut materials for a landscape bed. A power cutter with a diamond wheel like the Husqvarna Vari-Cut S45 handles those jobs.',
            1 => 'The S45 sits in Husqvarna\'s Vari-Cut range as a general-purpose blade. It is meant for users who cut a mix of materials occasionally rather than specialists cutting one material all day.',
            2 => 'If you only need to make a few cuts a year, renting a power cutter and buying a suitable blade may make more sense than owning the machine. If your crew does hardscape work regularly, owning a cutter and keeping a general-purpose blade on hand saves trips and rental fees.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Matching the blade to the saw',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Diamond blades must match the machine. Check that the blade diameter, arbor size and maximum operating speed all match or exceed what your power cutter requires. Never run a blade on a machine that can spin it faster than its rated maximum speed. Mount it in the correct rotation direction, using the arrow on the blade.',
            1 => 'The blade guard must be in place and adjusted correctly. If the blade does not fit the saw properly, do not use it.',
            2 => 'Store blades flat or hung in a dry place, away from heavy tools that could bend the core. A blade with a bent core or a damaged arbor hole can wobble and is not safe to use.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Dust control and safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Cutting concrete, masonry and stone creates respirable crystalline silica dust, which can cause serious lung disease. OSHA has a specific silica standard for construction. Use wet cutting or approved dust control methods, and wear appropriate respiratory protection as required. Wet cutting also keeps the blade cooler and extends its life.',
            1 => 'Wear eye and face protection, hearing protection, gloves and sturdy boots. Keep a firm stance, keep bystanders well back and watch for binding or pinching as the cut progresses. Power cutters can kick back. Only trained operators should use them, following the machine\'s operator manual.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Blade care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Let the blade do the work. Forcing it causes overheating and can glaze the segments, which slows cutting. If a blade stops cutting well, it may need dressing by making a few cuts in an abrasive material. Inspect the blade before each use for cracks, missing segments or damage to the core, and replace a damaged blade immediately.',
            1 => 'Browse more <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/">power equipment parts</a> for other wear items.',
            2 => 'Keep track of how much the segments have worn. When the diamond segments are close to the core, the blade is near the end of its life and should be replaced. Never use a blade with cracked or missing segments.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What does the Husqvarna Vari-Cut S45 cut?',
          'a' => 'It is a general-purpose diamond blade for concrete, masonry and other building materials. Check the listing for specific materials.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will it fit my power cutter?',
          'a' => 'Check the diameter, arbor size and maximum speed rating against your saw before using it.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I need to cut wet?',
          'a' => 'Wet cutting or other approved dust control is strongly recommended to control silica dust, and it extends blade life.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'ground',
        1 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/husqvarna/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/parts-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/parts-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Sharpening Chainsaw Blades',
        'creator' => 'NCDOTcommunications',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/39320593@N03',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/39320593@N03/36914543386',
      ),
    ),
    '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/bracing/bracing-rods/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/bracing/bracing-rods/',
      'category' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/',
      'name' => 'Tree Bracing Rods',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'cable-hardware',
      'price' => 25,
      'keyword' => 'rod bracing',
      'title' => 'Tree Bracing Rods for Rod Bracing',
      'h1' => 'Tree Bracing Rods for Rod Bracing',
      'meta' => 'Tree bracing rods for rod bracing weak unions and split limbs. How bracing works, how it pairs with cabling and why it needs a qualified arborist.',
      'tagline' => 'Threaded rods for bracing weak unions and split stems in trees.',
      'summary' => 'These tree bracing rods are threaded steel rods used for rod bracing, a technique that ties weak unions or split stems together to reduce movement. Bracing is usually paired with cabling higher in the tree for a complete support system.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Threaded steel rods for tree bracing',
        1 => 'Used with matching nuts and washers',
        2 => 'Helps stabilize weak unions and splits',
        3 => 'Often paired with cabling higher in the crown',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Threaded bracing rod',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'Steel',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Tree bracing per ANSI A300 Part 3',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Hardware',
          1 => 'Use matching nuts and washers',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How rod bracing works',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Rod bracing runs a threaded rod through two stems or across a split, with washers and nuts on each end. The rod limits movement between the parts, which can reduce stress on a weak union or hold a split together. It is used close to the weakness, while cables installed higher in the crown limit how far the stems can move apart.',
            1 => 'Bracing does not make a tree safe on its own. It is one tool in a support system, and it only makes sense when a qualified arborist has assessed the tree and decided the system is appropriate.',
            2 => 'Searches for braces rods and rod bracing come from both homeowners and arborists. If you are a homeowner with a split or weak union, the first step is a tree risk assessment by a qualified arborist, not hardware.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Installation and standards',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Tree support systems should be designed and installed by a qualified arborist following ANSI A300 (Part 3) and ANSI Z133, and inspected regularly once installed.',
            1 => 'Use rods, nuts and washers of matching size and grade, and drill holes to the correct size. Choose the rod diameter based on the stem size and the system design. Document the installation and schedule regular inspections. Browse more <a href="/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/">cabling and bracing</a> hardware.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is rod bracing used for?',
          'a' => 'It stabilizes weak unions or splits by tying the stems together with a threaded rod, usually as part of a system that includes cables.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can a homeowner install bracing rods?',
          'a' => 'Bracing should be designed and installed by a qualified arborist following ANSI A300 Part 3.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Stainless steel eye-bolt',
        'creator' => 'Unknown',
        'creator_url' => '',
        'license' => 'CC0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.rawpixel.com/image/6032390/photo-image-public-domain-free-silver',
      ),
