    '/rope/climbing-rope/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rope/climbing-rope/',
      'name' => 'Climbing Rope',
      'h1' => 'Tree Climbing Rope',
      'keyword' => 'tree climbing rope',
      'title' => 'Tree Climbing Rope: Arborist Climbing Lines by Type',
      'meta' => 'Tree climbing rope for arborists: 16-strand, 24-strand and static climbing lines, plus a rope placement tool, with tips on diameter, length and inspection.',
      'kind' => 'rope',
      'intro' => 'Tree climbing rope is life-support gear, so it is worth choosing carefully. Arborist climbing rope comes in 16-strand, 24-strand and static constructions, each with its own feel with hitches and devices.',
      'guide' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing arborist climbing rope',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Start with your system. Traditional doubled-rope climbing with a friction hitch works well with 16-strand rope. Mechanical devices and SRS often favor a round, firm 24-strand or static line in a specific diameter. Check your devices\' instructions for compatible ropes.',
            1 => 'Length should be at least twice your highest tie-in for doubled rope, with extra to spare. Inspect before every climb, keep the rope away from saws and fuel, and follow ANSI Z133 and the maker\'s instructions. Get trained before you climb.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Rope types at a glance',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Sixteen-strand ropes have thick covers that resist abrasion and play well with traditional friction hitches. Twenty-four strand ropes are round and firm, which suits devices and SRS. Static lines have the least stretch for efficient rope ascents and are common in stationary systems.',
            1 => 'Rope accessories round out a climbing kit: a throw line and weight to set your rope, a rope bag to store it, a chafe sleeve for rough tie-ins and a rope placement tool for reaching high anchors from the ground. Build your system as a whole so every part is compatible.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How long should my tree climbing rope be?',
          'a' => 'For a doubled rope system you need at least twice your tie-in height, plus extra. Many climbers start with 150 feet.',
        ),
      ),
      'parent' => '/rope/',
