    '/rope/climbing-rope/16-strand-climbing-rope/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rope/climbing-rope/16-strand-climbing-rope/',
      'name' => '16-Strand Rope',
      'h1' => '16-Strand Climbing Rope',
      'keyword' => '16-strand climbing rope',
      'title' => '16-Strand Climbing Rope for Tree Climbers',
      'meta' => '16-strand climbing rope for arborists: durable, hitch-friendly classic climbing lines with a thick cover that handles bark, friction hitches and daily use.',
      'kind' => 'rope',
      'intro' => 'A 16-strand climbing rope has a heavy cover braided from 16 strands over a lightly twisted core. It is a classic arborist line, durable and easy to grip with traditional friction hitches.',
      'guide' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why climbers choose 16-strand',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The thick cover stands up to bark and abrasion, and the rope tends to be forgiving with hitches like the Blake\'s hitch. It is a solid choice for doubled-rope climbing and new climbers learning a traditional system.',
            1 => 'Some 16-strand ropes are a little stiffer or have more stretch than modern constructions, which can matter on SRS devices. Confirm compatibility with your gear and inspect the rope before every climb.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Care and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A 16-strand cover will fuzz with use, which is normal. What is not normal is a glazed, stiff or flat section, exposed core, or any cut through the cover. Run the rope through your hands before every climb and retire it if you find damage or are unsure.',
            1 => 'Store the rope clean and dry in a rope bag out of sunlight, and keep it away from fuel, oils and chemicals. Many climbers mark their lines with the date in service and keep a simple log. Follow the manufacturer\'s instructions and ANSI Z133 for life-support rope.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is 16-strand rope good for SRS?',
          'a' => 'Some climbers use it, but many SRS devices are designed for specific rope types and diameters. Check the device instructions first.',
        ),
      ),
