    '/safety/communication/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/safety/communication/',
      'name' => 'Communication',
      'h1' => 'Arborist Communication Systems',
      'keyword' => 'arborist communication system',
      'title' => 'Arborist Communication System: Bluetooth Helmet Headsets',
      'meta' => 'Arborist communication system options: Bluetooth helmet headsets, mesh intercom and FRS radio ear muffs that let climbers and ground crews talk over saws.',
      'kind' => 'helmet',
      'intro' => 'An arborist communication system lets the climber and ground crew talk clearly over saws and chippers. Choose a Bluetooth helmet headset with mesh intercom, an integrated helmet system or radio ear muffs.',
      'guide' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why crews use helmet communication',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Rigging, felling and aerial rescue all depend on clear commands. Hand signals and shouting work until a chipper is running or the climber is out of sight. A headset on each helmet lets the crew confirm commands like stand clear, ready to cut and let it run before anything moves.',
            1 => 'Many crews still use a clear call-and-response system, even with headsets. Communication gear supports good procedures; it does not replace them.',
            2 => 'Communication also helps ground crew warn the climber about hazards they cannot see from above, such as a car pulling into the drop zone, a pedestrian on the sidewalk or a rope fouled on a fence. That shared awareness is one of the biggest safety benefits of a headset system.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Bluetooth and mesh intercom',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Bluetooth headsets pair directly between a few users. Mesh intercom systems connect multiple helmets in a group without pairing each one, and they handle people moving in and out of range more gracefully. Look at range, group size and battery life for a full day.',
            1 => 'Range and battery life vary between systems. Check the manufacturer\'s stated figures, but expect real-world range to be lower among trees, buildings and running equipment. Charge every unit overnight and keep a charging cable in the truck.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Integrated helmet systems and radio muffs',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'An integrated arborist helmet with Bluetooth or a communication kit built for a specific helmet keeps cables tidy and puts speakers inside hearing protection. Radio ear muffs with FRS radios work across mixed crews and longer distances where licensed or license-free radio channels apply.',
            1 => 'Make sure the system\'s ear cups still provide the hearing protection your crew needs, and keep volume at levels that do not add noise exposure.',
            2 => 'Some arborist helmets offer a dedicated communication kit designed to fit their shell and ear muffs. These kits often give the cleanest install and the best audio quality. Check whether your helmet maker offers one before choosing an aftermarket system.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing a system',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check compatibility with your helmets before buying, think about how many people need to talk at once, and plan for charging. Test the system at the start of each day.',
            1 => 'Train every crew member on the system\'s controls, especially how to mute, change volume and reconnect. A headset that only one person knows how to use will not help in an emergency. Set agreed-upon call signs and commands so everyone knows who is speaking and what the call means.',
            2 => 'Plan for weather, too. Many systems handle light rain, but check the manufacturer\'s water resistance rating before working in heavy rain. Keep spare batteries or a power bank in the truck, and mark each headset with a crew member\'s name so units do not get swapped between helmets with different settings.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I add communication to my existing arborist helmet?',
          'a' => 'Many systems mount to popular helmets or their ear muffs. Check the compatibility list before you buy.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How many people can share a mesh intercom?',
          'a' => 'It depends on the system. Mesh intercoms are designed for groups larger than Bluetooth pairing allows.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do communication headsets provide hearing protection?',
          'a' => 'Some systems are built into rated ear muffs. Others are speakers only, so pair them with proper hearing protection.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do headsets replace hand signals?',
          'a' => 'No. Keep a backup system of signals and clear commands.',
        ),
