    '/climbing/saddles-and-harnesses/weaver-saddles/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/saddles-and-harnesses/weaver-saddles/',
      'name' => 'Weaver Arborist Saddles',
      'h1' => 'Weaver Arborist Saddles',
      'keyword' => 'weaver arborist',
      'title' => 'Weaver Arborist Saddles: Cougar and Climbing Saddles',
      'meta' => 'Weaver arborist saddles, including the Cougar, are proven, affordable climbing saddles for tree work. Compare models, fit tips and typical street prices.',
      'kind' => 'saddle',
      'intro' => 'Weaver arborist saddles are proven, affordable climbing saddles made by a long-time US leather and webbing maker. From the popular Weaver Cougar saddle to simpler models, they are a common choice for new climbers and crews outfitting several people.',
      'guide' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why Weaver saddles are still around',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Weaver has been making climbing saddles for a long time, and you will find them on crews everywhere. They are straightforward, durable and priced so a crew can outfit new climbers without breaking the budget. For a lot of arborists, a Weaver saddle was the first one they climbed in.',
            1 => 'The Weaver Cougar saddle is a popular model, with padding and support that suits everyday pruning and removal work.',
            2 => 'Weaver saddles are also a common pick for crews that need spare saddles in the truck. When a climber\'s saddle is out for repair or a new hire starts, having a straightforward, well-fitting saddle on hand keeps the job moving.',
            3 => 'Weaver saddles are also easy to service. Webbing, leather and hardware are simple to inspect, and replacement parts are widely available for many models, which helps keep a saddle in use for its full service life.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing a Weaver climbing saddle',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Look at how you climb. If you mostly use a moving rope system and a flip line, a traditional saddle with side D-rings and a front tie-in covers the basics well. If you plan to climb SRS a lot or want a bridge that moves with you, compare with European saddles such as the <a href="/climbing/saddles-and-harnesses/european-saddles/petzl-sequoia-climbing-harness/">Petzl Sequoia</a>.',
            1 => 'Fit matters more than features. Use Weaver\'s sizing guide for waist and legs, and try the saddle on with the clothes you climb in.',
            2 => 'If you climb a lot of different trees and use a flip line often, side D-rings are especially important. Make sure they sit comfortably on your hips and do not dig in when you lean back on the lanyard.',
            3 => 'If you are buying for a crew, consider sizing a few climbers at once. A saddle that fits well makes new climbers more confident and more productive, and it helps them focus on technique instead of discomfort.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Adding to your saddle',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Many climbers add a chest harness, such as the <a href="/climbing/saddles-and-harnesses/saddle-parts-and-shoulder-harnesses/weaver-chest-box/">Weaver Chest Box</a>, when they start ascending stationary rope. Tool holders and gear loops help organize a rack. Check that any accessory works with your saddle and does not interfere with life-support components.',
            1 => 'Hang test any new setup close to the ground before climbing.',
            2 => 'Accessories can also make an older saddle more comfortable. Padding upgrades and tool holders can extend the life of a saddle you already like.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and retirement',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Inspect webbing, stitching, leather, buckles and D-rings before every climb. Look for cuts, abrasion, chemical contamination and cracked hardware. Retire the saddle when it shows significant wear, after a fall or when it reaches the maker\'s service life.',
            1 => 'A saddle is life-support equipment: buy one that meets the applicable standard, follow the manufacturer\'s instructions and ANSI Z133, inspect it before every climb and retire it when wear reaches the maker\'s limits. Get trained before you climb on any new system.',
            2 => 'Keep a simple log of when you put the saddle into service and any parts you replaced. It makes inspection easier and helps you plan replacement.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is a Weaver saddle good for beginners?',
          'a' => 'Many new climbers start in a Weaver saddle because they are affordable and straightforward. Fit and proper training matter more than brand.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the Weaver Cougar saddle?',
          'a' => 'It is one of Weaver\'s popular arborist climbing saddles, with padding and side D-rings for work positioning.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a Weaver saddle for SRS?',
          'a' => 'Many climbers do with the right setup, often adding a chest harness. Follow the manufacturer\'s instructions and get trained.',
        ),
      ),
      'products' => 
      array (
        0 => '/climbing/saddles-and-harnesses/saddle-parts-and-shoulder-harnesses/weaver-chest-box/',
      ),
      'children' => 
      array (
      ),
      'parent' => '/climbing/saddles-and-harnesses/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/saddle-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/saddle-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Saving Tyr 6',
        'creator' => 'ToobyDoo',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/7977078@N02',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/7977078@N02/2876232015',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/spurs/lightweight-spurs/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/spurs/lightweight-spurs/',
      'name' => 'Lightweight Tree Climbing Spurs',
      'h1' => 'Lightweight Tree Spikes and Climbing Spurs',
      'keyword' => 'tree spikes',
      'title' => 'Lightweight Tree Spikes: Climbing Spurs for Removals',
      'meta' => 'Lightweight tree spikes for removal climbers: what to look for in aluminum climbing spurs, pads, gaffs and boots, plus fit and safety tips. Typical price here.',
      'kind' => 'spur',
      'intro' => 'Lightweight tree spikes cut the weight you lift with every step, which makes a real difference on big removals. This guide covers what to look for in lightweight climbing spurs, from shank material to pads, gaffs and boots.',
      'guide' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why go light',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'On a big removal you might spend most of the day on spurs. Every ounce on your ankle is lifted on each step, so lightweight tree climbing spurs reduce fatigue in your hips, knees and calves. Climbers who switch from heavy steel usually notice it the first afternoon.',
            1 => 'The trade-off is cost and toughness. Light alloy shanks cost more and can dent or bend if dropped on rock or thrown in a truck bed. Treat them like the tool they are and they will last.',
            2 => 'Lightweight climbing spikes also help on travel-heavy work like storm cleanup, where you may hike gear into a back lot or carry it up a slope. Less weight in the bag makes the whole day easier.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What to look for in climbing spurs',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Start with pads. A wide, well-padded upper spreads load and keeps the shank aligned. Next look at gaffs: replaceable tree gaffs let you swap worn points without buying new climbers. Then check adjustment range so the top of the shank sits just below the bend of your knee.',
            1 => 'Straps should be easy to adjust with gloves on and easy to replace. Compare options in <a href="/climbing/spurs/aluminum-spurs/">aluminum spurs</a> and <a href="/climbing/spurs/buckingham-steel-spurs/">steel spurs</a> before deciding.',
            2 => 'Look at how the climbing spurs fasten as well. Buckles are secure and easy to adjust with gloves. Hook-and-loop closures are faster but lose grip when packed with sawdust. Pick the style that matches how often you get in and out of your spurs.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Boots and fit',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Tree climber spikes need a stiff boot with a steel shank in the sole. The stirrup sits under the arch, and a soft sole lets it fold your foot. High ankle support helps when you are standing sideways on a stem.',
            1 => 'Snug the lower strap so the stirrup cannot shift, and set the upper strap firm without cutting off circulation.',
            2 => 'Before buying, measure from the floor to just below the bend of your knee while wearing your boots. That tells you whether a spur\'s adjustment range fits your leg. Climbers with very long or short legs should check this carefully.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Safety and tree health',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Spurs are for removals and emergency rescue only. Every gaff hole wounds the tree, so climb trees you are keeping on rope. Always work with a flip line or steel-core lanyard and keep two points of attachment when cutting.',
            1 => 'Climbing spurs are part of your fall-protection system. Inspect gaffs, shanks, straps and pads before every climb, follow the manufacturer\'s instructions and ANSI Z133, and get trained before you climb on spikes.',
            2 => 'Store spurs with gaff guards on, keep them away from other tools that can bend gaffs, and sharpen with a file only. Replace gaffs, straps and pads as they wear so the whole system stays dependable.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How much lighter are aluminum spurs than steel?',
          'a' => 'It varies by model and pads, but aluminum shanks are noticeably lighter than steel. Check the maker\'s listed weight for the exact set you are considering.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Are lightweight spurs strong enough?',
          'a' => 'Spurs from reputable makers are built for climbing loads. Light alloys dent more easily than steel, so inspect them before each climb and retire damaged parts.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use tree spikes for pruning?',
          'a' => 'No. Use spurs only for removals or rescue. Prune live trees from a rope and saddle system.',
        ),
      ),
      'products' => 
      array (
        0 => '/climber-pads/replacement-pads-for-gecko-climbers/',
        1 => '/climbing/spurs/aluminum-spurs/climb-right-aluminum-climbers-with-big-buck-pads/',
        2 => '/climbing/spurs/aluminum-spurs/climb-right-aluminum-climbers-with-steel-support-pads/',
        3 => '/climbing/spurs/buckingham-steel-spurs/buckingham-steel-climbers-with-big-buck-pads/',
        4 => '/climbing/spurs/buckingham-steel-spurs/buckingham-steel-climbers-with-lower-straps/',
        5 => '/climbing/spurs/buckingham-steel-spurs/buckingham-steel-climbers-with-t-pads/',
        6 => '/climbing/spurs/climber-pads/buckingham-big-buck-pads/',
        7 => '/climbing/spurs/climber-pads/buckingham-big-buck-velcro-pads/',
        8 => '/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/buckalloy-screw-and-dowel-style-replacement-tree-gaffs/',
        9 => '/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/edelrid-talon-upper-strap-system/',
        10 => '/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/distel-replacement-pads-with-rings-and-straps/',
        11 => '/spurs/notch-ergo-climbers/',
      ),
      'children' => 
      array (
      ),
      'parent' => '/climbing/spurs/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/spur-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/spur-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Fall Protection Training, 2013',
        'creator' => 'Western Area Power Admin',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05/8813827776',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/mechanical-friction-devices/mrs-climbing-devices/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/mechanical-friction-devices/mrs-climbing-devices/',
      'name' => 'MRS Climbing Devices',
      'h1' => 'MRS Climbing Devices',
      'keyword' => 'mrs climbing kit',
      'title' => 'MRS Climbing Kit and Moving Rope Friction Devices',
      'meta' => 'Build an MRS climbing kit around a mechanical friction device for moving rope work. How MRS devices compare with hitches, what to pair them with and prices.',
      'kind' => 'friction-device',
      'intro' => 'An MRS climbing kit built around a mechanical friction device replaces or supplements the traditional friction hitch for moving rope work. These devices give consistent grab and release on a doubled line, with less cord to maintain and less heat build-up on long descents.',
      'guide' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What an MRS device does',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'In moving rope climbing, your line runs from your saddle, up over an anchor and back down to your friction system. The climber advances by pulling slack through the system. For decades that meant a friction hitch and a hitch-tending pulley. Mechanical MRS devices do the same job with metal parts that grip the rope through a cam, chain or bend path.',
            1 => 'The appeal is consistency. A hitch can grab differently when cord is new, worn, wet or hot. A mechanical device behaves the same from the first climb of the morning to the last, as long as it is used on a compatible rope and kept clean.',
            2 => 'Mechanical devices also reduce the heat that builds up on long descents, which helps protect your climbing line and gives you smoother control when you are lowering yourself out of a big tree.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Building an MRS climbing kit',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Start with the device and check its approved rope diameters and rope types against your climb line. Then add connectors the manufacturer approves, a small pulley or swivel if the device is designed for one, and a friction saver or ring set for the anchor. Rings from our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/rings/">rings</a> section are a common choice.',
            1 => 'Many climbers keep a friction hitch setup as a backup and for rescue scenarios. Knowing both systems makes you a more flexible climber.',
            2 => 'If you plan to move between moving and stationary rope, look for a device that is approved for both. Converting between modes usually means adding or rearranging components, and the steps must follow the manual exactly.',
            3 => 'Write down what you build and keep it the same. A consistent setup is easier to inspect and easier for a crew partner to check during a buddy check or a rescue.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing a device',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Think about how you climb most. Long limb walks and big spreading trees reward a device with very fine descent control. Fast production climbing on smaller trees rewards something quick to install and easy to tend. Weight, how easily the device releases with gloves on, and how well it handles a dirty rope all matter on a real job.',
            1 => 'Ask other climbers on your crew what they run, and try a device before you buy if you can. For stationary rope options, see our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/mechanical-friction-devices/srs-climbing-devices/">SRS climbing devices</a> page.',
            2 => 'Price matters too. A good MRS device is a real investment, so it pays to choose one that fits your rope, your trees and your style rather than chasing the newest design.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Training and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Mechanical devices change how your system behaves under load, and misrigging can be serious. Learn a new device from a qualified trainer, rig it on the ground, and test it before every climb. Keep it clean of sap and grit, check moving parts for wear, and retire it if anything is cracked, bent or worn past the maker\'s limits.',
            1 => 'Anything you hang your life on must meet the applicable standard, be used per the manufacturer\'s instructions and ANSI Z133, and be inspected before every climb by a trained user.',
            2 => 'Keep the device\'s manual with your kit or saved on your phone, and recheck it whenever you change rope or connectors.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is a mechanical MRS device better than a friction hitch?',
          'a' => 'It is more consistent and needs less cord maintenance, but it costs more and has its own learning curve. Many climbers carry both.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can MRS devices be used for SRS?',
          'a' => 'Some devices are approved for both systems with the right setup. Check the manufacturer\'s instructions for the specific device.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What rope works with an MRS device?',
          'a' => 'Each device lists approved rope diameters and constructions. Using a rope outside that range can affect grab and control.',
        ),
      ),
      'products' => 
      array (
        0 => '/climbing/climbing-gear/ascent-and-descent/mechanical-friction-devices/devices-for-mrs/hitch-hiker-2/',
        1 => '/climbing/climbing-gear/ascent-and-descent/mechanical-friction-devices/devices-for-mrs/petzl-zigzag-mechanical-friction-device/',
        2 => '/devices-for-mrs/buckingham-throttle/',
      ),
      'children' => 
      array (
      ),
      'parent' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/friction-device-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/friction-device-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Crimea_IMG_9320 1',
        'creator' => 'kstepanoff',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/68732633@N04',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/68732633@N04/12693333643',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/mechanical-friction-devices/srs-climbing-devices/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/mechanical-friction-devices/srs-climbing-devices/',
      'name' => 'SRS Climbing Devices',
      'h1' => 'SRS Climbing Devices',
      'keyword' => 'mechanical prusik',
      'title' => 'SRS Climbing Devices and Mechanical Prusik Options',
      'meta' => 'Compare SRS climbing devices, from mechanical prusik units to rope wrench setups, for stationary rope tree work. What to look for and typical prices.',
      'kind' => 'friction-device',
      'intro' => 'SRS climbing devices, often called a mechanical prusik when they replace a friction hitch, control your descent and positioning on a single stationary line. They let a climber ascend efficiently and work the canopy on one rope anchored at the base or high in the tree.',
      'guide' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How SRS devices fit into a system',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'On a stationary rope the line does not move through the anchor; you move along it. That means your device does all the friction work, and it needs to give smooth control without excessive heat. Some setups use a friction hitch tended by a device, others use a fully mechanical unit. Ascent is handled with chest and foot ascenders or a rope-walking setup.',
            1 => 'A good SRS kit also includes a base anchor or retrievable canopy anchor, quality connectors and a rope within the device\'s rated range. See moving-rope options on our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/mechanical-friction-devices/mrs-climbing-devices/">MRS climbing devices</a> page.',
            2 => 'Stationary rope work also changes how you think about anchors. The anchor carries the full load on a single line, and a canopy anchor sees forces differently than a moving rope redirect. Choosing the anchor point and tie-off method is as important as choosing the device.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing and using one',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Look at rope compatibility, how the device handles a long descent, how easy it is to lock off for work positioning and how it behaves with gloves on. Learn SRS from a qualified trainer; anchoring and rescue are different from moving rope work.',
            1 => 'Anything you hang your life on must meet the applicable standard, be used per the manufacturer\'s instructions and ANSI Z133, and be inspected before every climb by a trained user.',
            2 => 'Many climbers keep both MRS and SRS gear in the truck and pick the system that suits each tree. Learning both makes you more flexible, and it gives you options for rescue.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
